window.OBT_ARTICLES=[{"title": "The Flat Earth Is a Trust Problem, Not a Science Problem", "subtitle": "Scientists keep answering flat-earthers with evidence. The evidence is right. It is not what is missing.", "topic": "Institutions & trust", "url": "/essays/the-flat-earth-is-a-trust-problem/", "text": "The physicist Brian Cox answered the flat-earther’s clever argument, granted that these people are not idiots, and stopped there.What does a man who believes the Earth is flat have to teach the institutions that run on evidence? More than they have been willing to learn from him.People who have the evidence on their side have a habit, and they cannot see it in themselves. When someone does not believe them, they explain again, with more evidence. This essay is about why that fails, and what it costs. The short version: the person who does not believe is not short of facts. He has decided not to trust the people handing him the facts. More facts from those people are more of what he has already turned down.Earlier essays in this series split trust into two kinds. Assumed trust is the kind a person gives in advance, because the source is old, or official, or because he was taught to. Inferred trust is the kind he works out afterward, from what he can see of how the source behaves. [1] The argument here rests on that split, and it comes down to this. An institution whose legitimacy rests on accurate evidence cannot assume it is legitimate in anyone’s eyes because the evidence is accurate. Legitimacy is extended by the person, on the flat-earther’s terms, which are now everyone’s terms: can I see it, or do I trust the one who says he did? The institutions cannot show everyone everything with their own eyes. But they can stop assuming two things: that being right will be enough, and that contempt is free. Under assumed trust, those habits cost little, because nobody was reading them. Under inferred trust, they are the evidence the other side is reading.Flat-earthers are the cleanest place to watch this, because nothing rides on the shape of the Earth. The same mistake, made where something does ride on it, has a bill attached. And the machines now answering everyone’s questions are about to make it universal.The glacierIn December 2024 a pastor from outside Denver took a group of flat-earthers to Antarctica to look at the sun. He paid the travel and equipment of four of them himself. [2] By his account, both sides had agreed on the test before they left. On the usual flat-earth map, Antarctica is a wall of ice around the rim of the world, and nowhere in the far south can the sun stay up for twenty-four hours. On a globe, in the southern summer, it does. So they went south, and at midnight the sun was still up, circling the sky.One of them had spent years making flat-earth videos. He said on camera that sometimes you are wrong in life, and that he had thought there was no twenty-four-hour sun. [3] Within weeks he said he was stepping away from the movement. [3] Another man in the group accepted the sun, kept the belief, and by spring was working on a new model that could hold both. [4]Decades of photographs from orbit had not done what that trip did for one man. And the trip did nothing for the other. The difference between those two men is what this essay is about.The test at the glacier. On the globe, the sun circles the sky and never sets. On the flat map it has to set. At midnight it was still up.The clever argumentIn a short video published in July 2026, the physicist Brian Cox takes on a flat-earth argument. [5] If the Earth spins once a day and also goes around the sun once a year, then six months from now the side facing the sun at noon should be facing away. Noon should have become midnight. It has not. So the Earth does not move, and the four minutes a day that astronomers add to the day are a patch to save the theory. Cox called the argument clever, and it is. The answer is that the four minutes are not a patch. They are the thing being observed. The Earth takes about twenty-three hours and fifty-six minutes to turn once against the stars, then about four minutes more to bring the sun back overhead, because it has moved a little way along its orbit in the meantime. The flat-earther saw something real and put it in the wrong frame.Cox says one more thing in the clip, and it matters more than the astronomy. “These people,” he says, “they’re not idiots. What they’re doing is focusing on one thing ... and then being suspicious about the rest.” [5] That is further than the evidence side usually gets. He grants the man his intelligence, and he names the suspicion. Notice where he stops. The suspicion is treated as a habit of mind, a fault in how the man looks at things, and that is where it is left. Nobody asks what the rest is, who supplied it, or why the man stopped taking their word for it. The rest is everything he was asked to believe on someone else’s say-so. That is the question this essay is about, and the four minutes cannot answer it.What happens next for the person Cox is answering is usually nothing. It is likely as hard for a flat-earther to fathom what Brian Cox is saying as it is for Brian Cox to fathom what the flat-earther believes. They are looking at the same sky through two different instruments.Cox decides what is true the way a scientist does. Run the test. If anyone who runs it gets the same answer, the claim stands, no matter who made it. A bishop and a clerk look through the same telescope and see the same moon. That is the beauty of the method. It swaps the question of whom to believe for a question anyone can settle. But most people will never run the test. They take it on faith that the people who ran it did it honestly. The method does not get rid of trust. It moves it, from the man making the claim to the people running the test.The flat-earther decides what is true the older way. He believes what he can see with his own eyes, and what people he trusts have seen with theirs. That is how most people have decided most things for most of history, and it works until the thing in question is too big to see from where you stand. Then it comes down to one question: do you trust the people who say they saw it? He has decided he does not. So Cox’s explanation arrives from the one source he has already ruled out. He is wrong about the sky; the four minutes prove it. But the four minutes cannot reach the real disagreement, which is not about the sky at all. The two men disagree about what counts as a reason to believe something.The scriptFor a hundred and fifty years the people with the evidence have answered flat-earthers by supplying more of it. That is understandable, since evidence is what they have, and it is right, since the evidence is true. It has also not ended the movement. In 1870 a flat-earther bet five hundred pounds that six miles of canal would show no curve. He lost in front of an agreed umpire, and spent the next five years accusing the winner of fraud. [6] In the 2018 documentary Behind the Curve, flat-earthers test a gyroscope sensitive enough to feel the Earth turn. It shows fifteen degrees of drift an hour, exactly what a turning Earth should give. The man running it says on camera that they were “not willing to accept that,” and they go looking for another cause of the drift, in the heavens rather than in the Earth. [7] The reading was accepted. The explanation was moved somewhere nobody could check.The pattern is so regular it can be written as a script. A believer makes a claim. A scientist explains, correctly, why it is wrong. The believer does not accept the explanation. The scientist, or more often the scientist’s audience, mocks him. The believer goes home having learned two things: that the round Earth comes with contempt attached, and that the people who hold it were never going to take him seriously. Both confirm the one thing he was already sure of. The institutions are not on his side.The script. Evidence enters at step two. Trust leaves at step four.This is not a claim that evidence never works. On most questions, for most people, a correction moves them a little toward the fact. [8] The flat-earther who is still a flat-earther is the limiting case. The correction reaches him from a source whose word is worth nothing to him, and more evidence from that source cannot close a gap that is about the source.Being right is necessary. It is not the same as being believed.Held on creditHere is the fact the whole debate keeps walking past. Nobody checks that the Earth is round. A few hundred people have seen it from orbit. Everyone else holds it the way they hold most of what they know: on credit. School said so. The map says so. The pilot, the satellite that finds the phone, every government on Earth. [9] It may be the most institutional fact there is, believed by nearly everyone and checked by nearly no one. That is assumed trust, in its purest form.The flat-earther has called in that credit, and the record shows he did not start with the Earth. When researchers interviewed thirty people at the first flat-earth conference in 2017, the lead researcher later said that twenty-nine had come to it through YouTube, and most had got there from videos about other things first: the moon landings, the attacks of 2001, a school shooting. [10] The flat earth was the last thing their distrust reached, not the first. That is how credit works. It is not withdrawn one fact at a time. It is withdrawn from the lender, and then everything that lender ever supplied comes due at once.What replaces it is the oldest rule there is: believe what you can see. The horizon looks flat. Water lies level. A man who keeps that rule, and lets the horizon decide what he looks at, will reason his way to a flat earth honestly, from the only evidence he still accepts. He is a skeptic with the institutions taken out. The religious believer in the movement, and there are many, has not stopped trusting either. He has moved his trust from the agency to the book. The question was never whether a person trusts. It is whom.Notice how little rides on this. No policy turns on the shape of the Earth, and nobody’s health does. What a believer has at stake is social: a community, a standing in it. Those are stakes in belonging, not in the fact. Take them out and what is left is the mechanism by itself. It does not stay where it costs nothing.The billIn 2020 the United States ran a crash program for a vaccine. What follows is an anecdote, not a measurement, and it should be read as one. In September 2020, with the trials still running, one host of The View said she would take the vaccine after the president’s daughter took it, and another asked whether the agency that approves vaccines had been politicized so a vaccine could appear right before the election. [11] Fourteen months later, with a different hand on the same agency and the same vaccine, the same table told an unvaccinated guest that she was putting her personal freedom ahead of other people’s safety, and that the show should not allow such misinformation on its air. [12] The hosts were not lying in either year, and this essay does not say they were. This author reads the contrast as inferred trust, sensitive to whose hand is on the institution. KFF polling also found partisan differences. That September, 85 percent of Democrats and 35 percent of Republicans worried that political pressure would rush FDA approval before a vaccine’s safety and effectiveness had been established. That concern about approval was not itself a refusal to be vaccinated. By October 2021, party identification, including independents who leaned toward a party, was the strongest predictor among the variables KFF modeled of whether an adult reported receiving at least one dose. [13] Those were different questions in different samples. They did not show the same people reversing their trust, or establish that a change of administration caused anyone to do so.Opinion 1. Unlike the flat earth, this one came with a bill. A study linked 538,159 all-cause deaths during 2018–2021 among voters aged 25 or older in Florida and Ohio to their 2017 Republican or Democratic voter records. The two groups’ excess death rates were similar before vaccine eligibility widened. In the period from spring through the end of 2021, after eligibility opened to all adults, the adjusted excess death rate among Republican voters was 43 percent higher than among Democratic voters. The difference was primarily seen in Ohio. [14] The study had no individual vaccination or trust data and did not establish what caused the gap. The reading is this author’s: trust may be part of the explanation, but these results do not prove it.The age of AIThe earlier essays argued that trust in AI is checked now, not assumed. [1] The machines that answer questions are the biggest institution of evidence ever built. Ask one why the Earth is round and it gives the Cox answer at once, to anyone, for nothing. By this essay’s argument, that is the problem, not the cure. A model has no name, no money at risk, no test it agreed to beforehand, and no sun to stand under. And because the machines are built to agree, as an earlier essay argued, each side will rent its own expert, and the argument in the language of evidence becomes free, instant and endless. The believer’s rule, believe what you can see, will break where it is weakest, in anything that arrives on a screen, once a video of a midnight sun can be made in an afternoon by someone who has never seen one. For anyone not standing under the sun, which is nearly everyone about nearly everything, what is left is the oldest question: whom do I trust. The age of AI does not make the flat-earther a relic. It makes him a preview.Opinion 2. The obvious answer is to forbid it. Build the machines so they will not help anyone argue against what science has settled. This author thinks that cure is worse than the disease. Most of what the method can address is not settled, and the line between settled and open is a judgment that has been wrong before. A rule against questioning settled things is a rule against the method, enforced by a company. Worse, it puts the labs in the chair of deciding what is true for everyone who asks, which is the one chair no institution of evidence should want, because it turns a trust problem into a power problem. A machine that refused the flat-earther would not persuade him. It would confirm everything he already thought about the people behind it.What stands betweenThe pastor had three things no space agency has: a name, his own money on the line, and a test agreed before anyone knew the result. Then he stood under the same sun. One man changed his mind and one did not, so that is not a recipe. But it is the whole difference between evidence about the fact and evidence about the people, and trust is built on the second. None of it made the pastor right. It made him answerable, and that is what a person needs before he will believe.Opinion 3. That is what stands between institutions of evidence and institutions of belief. Not a fact; nobody is short of facts. Being right is necessary. It is not the same as being believed. The institutions cannot take everyone to the pole, and they cannot make anyone trust them. What they can do is stop handing the other side its evidence: the explanation offered as if it settled the matter, and the contempt that follows when it does not. Under inferred trust, both get read.What would an institution have to do to earn your trust back? Share your view in the comments. Subscribe free to On Borrowed Times for more essays on institutions, merit and AI.ReferencesEarlier essays in this series: “Data Centers Have Exposed Big Tech’s Trust Deficit,” October 2026, https://johnelijah1.substack.com/p/data-centers-have-exposed-big-techs, and “Trust in AI Is Checked Now, Not Assumed,” October 2026, https://johnelijah1.substack.com/p/trust-in-ai-is-checked-now-not-assumedThe Final Experiment, official site (FAQ on the organizer, Union Glacier camp and the proposed test); Will Duffy interviewed in the Denver Post, January 17, 2025, https://www.denverpost.com/2025/01/17/flat-earth-final-experiment-antarctica-will-duffy/ (Duffy paid the travel and equipment of four participants; four flat-earth believers and four globe believers took part).Jeran Campanella, on camera at Union Glacier, in “It’s Midnight in Antarctica!”, December 2024; his intention to step away from the movement is reported in the Denver Post interview cited in [2].Austin Whitsitt, “Aether Cosmology and The Final Experiment,” The Way Forward podcast, episode 168, May 28, 2025, https://thewayfwrd.com/podcast/ep-168-aether-cosmology-and-the-final-experiment-with-austin-witsit/Brian Cox, clip published on YouTube, July 29, 2026, https://youtube.com/shorts/VQcb4-JD0s8. Both remarks quoted in the essay, that the argument is clever and that these people are not idiots, are in the clip. The uploader credits a 2016 National Gallery conversation as the source of the footage; the recording date has not been independently confirmed.Alfred Russel Wallace, My Life: A Record of Events and Opinions (Chapman and Hall, 1905), vol. II, pp. 364–374, https://darwin-online.org.uk/converted/Ancillary/1905_Wallace_A237/1905_Wallace_A237.2.html; Wallace, “The Hampden-Wallace Libel Case,” 1875, https://people.wku.edu/charles.smith/wallace/S248B.htm; the 1870 wager documents, https://people.wku.edu/charles.smith/wallace/S162-163.htm. The “five years” is Wallace’s own count in the 1875 statement.Behind the Curve, directed by Daniel J. Clark, 2018; the gyroscope scene at about 49:21 and 49:56.Thomas Wood and Ethan Porter, “The Elusive Backfire Effect: Mass Attitudes’ Steadfast Factual Adherence,” Political Behavior 41 (2019): 135–163, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11109-018-9443-y (five experiments, more than 10,100 participants, fifty-two issues, no issue on which the correction on average produced a backfire); Asheley R. Landrum, Alex Olshansky and Othello Richards, “Differential Susceptibility to Misleading Flat Earth Arguments on YouTube,” Media Psychology, 2021 (greater science knowledge reduced susceptibility, including among the conspiracy-minded). The conclusion that facts alone do not move a denier and engagement can is also Lee McIntyre’s, in How to Talk to a Science Denier (MIT Press, 2021), from the 2018 Flat Earth International Conference.The credit analogy for knowledge is William James’s, in Pragmatism, lecture VI, 1907; the point that the ordinary person holds the round Earth on the word of specialists was made by George Orwell in “As I Please,” Tribune, December 27, 1946.Asheley Landrum, interviewed in The Guardian, “Study blames YouTube for rise in number of Flat Earthers,” February 17, 2019, https://www.theguardian.com/science/2019/feb/17/study-blames-youtube-for-rise-in-number-of-flat-earthers; Alex Olshansky, master’s thesis on flat-earth beliefs, Texas Tech University, 2018 (the interview study).The View, ABC, September 2020. Joy Behar: “I will take the vaccine after Ivanka takes it,” reported by Mediaite, September 10, 2020, https://www.mediaite.com/tv/ivanka-trump-says-shell-take-coronavirus-vaccine-live-on-the-view-after-joy-behar-challenges-her-to-take-it/; Sunny Hostin, on the FDA having “been politicized” and whether a vaccine would be made available “right before the election,” September 9, 2020, as reported at https://dailycaller.com/2020/09/09/sunny-hostin-invokes-tuskegee-experiments-black-community-shouldnt-trust-trump-vaccineThe View, ABC, November 16, 2021. Sunny Hostin to an unvaccinated guest host: “I don’t understand why you would prioritize your personal freedom over the health and safety of others,” and “I don’t think we should allow this kind of misinformation on our air,” reported by Mediaite, https://www.mediaite.com/tv/the-view-goes-off-the-rails-after-jedediah-bila-defends-vaccine-mandate-opposition-youve-been-at-fox-tv-too-long/KFF Health Tracking Poll, August 28 to September 3, 2020, “Most Americans Worry Political Pressure Will Lead to Premature Approval of a COVID-19 Vaccine,” September 10, 2020 (62 percent worried overall; 85 percent of Democrats, 35 percent of Republicans); KFF COVID-19 Vaccine Monitor, “The Increasing Importance of Partisanship in Predicting COVID-19 Vaccination Status,” November 16, 2021 (survey of October 14 to 24, 2021; partisanship the strongest predictor of vaccination among the variables modeled).Jacob Wallace, Paul Goldsmith-Pinkham and Jason L. Schwartz, “Excess Death Rates for Republican and Democratic Registered Voters in Florida and Ohio During the COVID-19 Pandemic,” JAMA Internal Medicine, 2023, https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2023.1154 (538,159 deaths, 2018 to 2021; the 43 percent is the relative difference in excess death rates after April 1, 2021). The step from the Antarctic expedition to a lesson for public health was drawn earlier by Garrett Hollembeak, “What Flat Earthers Can Teach Public Health Experts,” Infection Control Today, March 6, 2025.Facts are sourced. Opinions are marked."}, {"title": "What The Economist Gets Wrong About AI and Jobs", "subtitle": "The jobs come back in the totals. They do not come back to the people who lost them.", "topic": "Work & economy", "url": "/essays/what-the-economist-gets-wrong-about/", "text": "The sum is a boom.The Economist has changed its mind about AI and jobs this year. In May it ran a leader headed “Prepare for an AI jobs apocalypse” [18]. In September it said the apocalypse was postponed and an AI jobs boom had begun [1]. This month it posted a short video. Americans, the video says, are gloomier about their job prospects than at any time since polling began, AI is the main reason, and the video asks whether they are right to worry. Its answer: “For now, try not to worry. The job apocalypse isn’t here yet” [2]. One line of history holds the whole argument up. “If AI were really to put millions of people out of work, it would be unprecedented,” the video says. “New technologies have never before destroyed jobs faster than they produce them” [2]. It is a comforting sentence. It is also not true.Even where the arithmetic behind the sentence holds, it answers a question nobody asked. The total number of jobs is the one number no person lives in. Technology does not take away totals. It takes particular jobs from particular people in particular places. The jobs it creates go, mostly, to other people, somewhere else, later. The Economist’s own figures show this. It adds up the columns, reports the sum, and stops. The sum is where the argument is safe. The columns are where the people are.This is not an argument against AI. This author’s view is that AI is a turning point and that there is no going back. It will do a great deal of good and a real amount of harm, and which is larger has not been settled. Nor is this an argument against churn. Jobs end and new jobs start. That is how economies grow, and no serious person wants it stopped. The argument is about the accounting. A technology this large will be trusted only as far as its costs are counted as carefully as its benefits. The Economist is counting the gains and not the losses. It is keeping half the ledger.Where The Economist is rightThe totals do not show a collapse. Unemployment was 4.2 percent in September, and the economy is still adding jobs, slowly [3]. Layoffs blamed on AI are real and rising: about 120,000 announced so far this year, now the most common stated reason for cuts [4]. Employers let go of roughly 1.7 million workers in an ordinary month, so that is still a small number [1]. The Economist’s estimate that AI has created about a million American jobs, against about 200,000 layoffs blamed on AI, may be generous. It is not absurd [1].The Economist is also right that panics about machines have a poor record. Keynes warned of technological unemployment in 1930. A committee of academics told the White House in 1964 that automation was breaking the link between work and income. Oxford researchers said in 2013 that nearly half of American jobs were at high risk, perhaps within a decade or two [5]. None of it has arrived. Over the long run, the record is one of new work, not no work. About six in ten American jobs in 2018 were kinds of work that did not exist in 1940 [6].So far, so fair. The apocalypse is not in the data. The Economist says so, and its own words are “postponed” and “for now,” and those two hedges deserve more attention than they get. A postponement is not a cancellation. A law of history that needs “for now” attached to it was never a law. “For now” is not a finding. It is a way out.Where the line is false“Never before” is a claim about every technology in every period. It fails on the record The Economist itself relies on. Economists have measured the two forces at work: machines pushing labor out of tasks, and new tasks bringing labor back in. In the United States from 1947 to 1987 the two were roughly balanced. From 1987 to 2017 they were not. The pushing out grew and the bringing back shrank. Labor demand grew at half its earlier pace, and in manufacturing the pushing out alone cut it by nearly a third [7]. That is thirty years, in the largest economy in the world, in which machines removed work faster than they added it. Thirty years is not a rough quarter. It is a working life. The video’s own example is the computer [2]. The thirty years after 1987 are the computer’s years.Labor demand, measured as the real wage bill per person, grew 2.5 percent a year from 1947 to 1987 and 1.3 percent a year from 1987 to 2017 [7]. The video’s example is the computer. These are the computer’s years.Then there is the case The Economist should like best, because the totals came out fine. In the early 1900s, telephone operator was one of the most common jobs for young American women. Between 1920 and 1940, AT&T replaced operators with mechanical switches across more than half the country and ended most of the jobs. The young women who came after found other work, and overall employment did not fall. The women who were operators when the switches arrived had a different outcome. A decade later they were more likely to be in lower-paid work or out of work altogether [8]. The total recovered. The operators did not.That is the pattern, and it is the pattern inside The Economist’s own September numbers. Since January 2023, customer service employment is down about 10 percent and administrative assistants about 15 percent. Hiring in professional services is running about a tenth below its pre-pandemic average. Meanwhile engineers, developers, and data scientists have added hundreds of thousands of jobs above trend. Installation and maintenance workers at data centers are being offered about 40 percent more than comparable work [1]. The Economist counted those columns, added them up, and called the sum a boom. The sum is a boom. The columns are a transfer from one group of people to another.Different jobs, different peopleThis is the point The Economist does not make. David Autor, the MIT economist behind the six-in-ten figure, put it in one sentence: “The real concern is for whom the new work has been created” [9]. Before 1980, new American work appeared mostly in middle-paid production and clerical jobs, the kind an ordinary worker could move into. After 1980 it appeared mostly in high-paid professional work, with low-paid service work second, while the clerical and production jobs in the middle kept being automated away [6]. The clerk whose job was automated was not hired as the software developer. Someone else was, usually younger, usually somewhere else. A net count says how many jobs there are. It does not say who lost one and who got one.What the displaced lose is known. One study followed men with at least three years on the job who lost it in a mass layoff. On average, counted over the next twenty years, they gave up more than a year of their old earnings when times were good. When times were bad, they gave up nearly three years [10]. A job created in another city, for another person, at another skill level, pays none of that back.An average is a true number that happens to no one.The AI version is already visible to anyone who looks below the average. A study published this month sorted American work into three kinds: what AI can do, what it can help with, and what it cannot touch. Since 2021 the first kind has been shrinking as a share of work and the second growing. The two have moved far enough in opposite directions that the total looks calm. The loss is not arriving as layoffs. It is arriving as hiring that does not happen. Job starts for workers in their early twenties in the most replaceable occupations have fallen by about a third. There is no such drop for the same age group in work AI cannot do [11]. In the second quarter of 2026, recent college graduates were unemployed at 5.6 percent, against about 4.3 percent for all workers in the same months [12][17]. The authors called their study “Hiding in the mean.” The Economist is reporting the mean and calling it the news.Since 2021, work AI can do has lost about 1.5 million jobs a year and work AI can help with has gained about 0.7 million a year, both measured against work AI does not touch [11]. The difference mostly shows up as hiring that does not happen.How technology disruptsThe Economist says the fear is overblown because the count is fine. That mistakes what the count measures. A new job replaces a lost one only if it is in the same place, at the same pay, open to the same person, at the same time. Those four conditions are almost never met together. The electrician’s job at the data center is real. It is not the customer service representative’s job, it is not in her town, and she is not an electrician. The loss happens to her in a single quarter. The new job happens to someone else over a decade. Added up across the country and across the years, the two cancel on paper. In the places and the lives where they land, they do not cancel at all.That is how technology disrupts. Not in totals, but in who, where, when, and at what pay. The question the video’s sentence should raise is the question it never reaches: created for whom, and how long is the gap.The rate of churnEvery past reassurance depended on time. The telephone switches took twenty years to reach half the country, and the operators they displaced were still worse off a decade later. Earlier machines took longer still. People had a working life, or at least a generation, to move. Generative AI is not moving at that speed. Two years after its public debut, about four in ten working-age Americans had used it. That is faster uptake than the personal computer or the internet [13]. The first measurable effects on hiring showed up within three years.Share of Americans aged 18 to 64 who had used the technology about two years after it arrived: generative AI about 40 percent in 2024, the internet about 20 percent at the same point, the personal computer about 20 percent after three years [13].Here the honest statement is a question, not a claim. Nobody knows yet whether the creation of new work has sped up to match. If losses now arrive in quarters and new work still arrives in decades, the totals can hold while the gap widens. The gap is where people live. The old comfort was never that nobody lost. It was that there was time. Whether there is still time is the question to watch, and it is exactly the question a net count hides.Unemployment in the second quarter of 2026: recent college graduates 5.6 percent, all workers about 4.3 percent [12] [17].An accounting, not a verdictThe jobs argument has a shape, and The Economist has used it before. In September it called the public backlash against data centers a foolish moral panic, “fuelled by misinformation and misunderstanding” [14]. Some of the water figures in circulation are wrong. Having established that, the piece stopped. It never asked why so many people, across the whole political spectrum, had become angry enough to show up at county hearings. This author gave an answer elsewhere. People used Big Tech’s products for twenty years without trusting the companies, and a data center is the first piece of the AI era they could vote on [15]. Calling a worry a myth does not end it. It tells the worried that nobody is listening.The same move, made about jobs, will get the same result. People can tell when they are being handed an average. An average is a true number that happens to no one. The customer service representative whose team stopped hiring in 2023 does not need a correspondent to explain that the totals are fine. The totals were never the question. The question is whether anyone is counting. AI has to be trusted if it is to do the good it can do. That takes a fair and open accounting: gains and losses, for whom, where, and how fast. Not a glossed version. Trust does not come from the people who say there is nothing to see. It comes from the people who show the whole count.The Economist is entitled to its view. It was founded in 1843 to argue for free trade [16], and this author reads its AI coverage as part of that tradition. Being for markets and for new technology is a respectable editorial position. The objection is not to the position. It is to an opinion being dressed up as a fact. “New technologies have never before destroyed jobs faster than they produce them” is not a finding. It is a point of view, delivered in the tone The Economist uses for settled facts, with “for now” added in case the data change. Confidence is the house style. It is not evidence.The honest version is available, and it is still optimistic. The totals are holding. The losses are falling on the young, the clerical, the customer-facing, and the towns that depend on them. The gains are going to engineers, electricians, and data scientists. History says the totals recover and the displaced mostly do not, and the gap between the two is measured in careers, not quarters. That version lets readers find themselves in the picture. The Economist’s version asks them to take comfort in an average that describes none of them.The jobs come back in the totals. They do not come back to the people who lost them.ReferencesThe Economist, “The jobs apocalypse is postponed. An AI jobs boom is here,” 4 September 2026. https://www.economist.com/finance-and-economics/2026/09/04/the-jobs-apocalypse-is-postponed-an-ai-jobs-boom-is-hereThe Economist, “Are you about to lose your job?” video with Alex Hern, 19 October 2026. U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, “The Employment Situation, September 2026,” released 2 October 2026. https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htmChallenger, Gray & Christmas, “Job Cuts Fall in September; Hiring Plans Up 3% Over 2025,” October 2026. https://www.challengergray.com/blog/job-cuts-fall-in-september-hiring-plans-up-3-over-2025-on-weak-early-seasonal-hiring/John Maynard Keynes, “Economic Possibilities for our Grandchildren,” 1930; Ad Hoc Committee on the Triple Revolution, memorandum to President Lyndon B. Johnson, 22 March 1964; Carl Benedikt Frey and Michael A. Osborne, “The Future of Employment: How Susceptible Are Jobs to Computerisation?” Oxford Martin School, 2013. https://oms-www.files.svdcdn.com/production/downloads/academic/The_Future_of_Employment.pdfDavid Autor, Caroline Chin, Anna Salomons and Bryan Seegmiller, “New Frontiers: The Origins and Content of New Work, 1940 to 2018,” Quarterly Journal of Economics 139, no. 3, 2024. https://www.nber.org/papers/w30389Daron Acemoglu and Pascual Restrepo, “Automation and New Tasks: How Technology Displaces and Reinstates Labor,” Journal of Economic Perspectives 33, no. 2, 2019. https://www.nber.org/papers/w25684James Feigenbaum and Daniel P. Gross, “Answering the Call of Automation: How the Labor Market Adjusted to Mechanizing Telephone Operation,” Quarterly Journal of Economics 139, no. 3, 2024. https://www.nber.org/papers/w28061Peter Dizikes, “Most work is ‘new’ work, U.S. Census data show,” MIT News, 1 April 2024, quoting David Autor. https://news.mit.edu/2024/most-work-is-new-work-us-census-data-shows-0401Steven J. Davis and Till von Wachter, “Recessions and the Costs of Job Loss,” Brookings Papers on Economic Activity, Fall 2011. https://www.nber.org/papers/w17638Benjamin Verschuere and Angus Cameron, “Hiding in the mean: AI is adding jobs and removing them at the same time,” VoxEU, Centre for Economic Policy Research, 3 October 2026. https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/hiding-mean-ai-adding-jobs-and-removing-them-same-timeFederal Reserve Bank of New York, “The Labor Market for Recent College Graduates,” data through the second quarter of 2026. https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-marketAlexander Bick, Adam Blandin and David Deming, “The Rapid Adoption of Generative AI,” Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, 23 September 2024. https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2024/sep/rapid-adoption-generative-aiThe Economist, “The moral panic over data centres is foolish,” 3 September 2026.John Elijah, “Data Centers Have Exposed Big Tech’s Trust Deficit,” On Borrowed Time, Substack, 13 October 2026. https://johnelijah1.substack.com/p/data-centers-have-exposed-big-techsThe Economist Group, “About us.” https://www.economistgroup.com/about-usU.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, civilian unemployment rate, series LNS14000000, April to June 2026, via FRED, Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/UNRATEThe Economist, “Prepare for an AI jobs apocalypse,” leader, 14 May 2026. https://www.economist.com/leaders/2026/05/14/prepare-for-an-ai-jobs-apocalypse"}, {"title": "Can Europe’s Social Model Save Itself?", "subtitle": "Most of Europe’s pension and care systems have no working way to correct course in time. Some built one and switched it off. One kept it.", "topic": "Europe & global affairs", "url": "/essays/can-europes-social-model-save-itself/", "text": "Do not adjust.Part oneAcross Europe, the systems that pay for pensions, health care, long-term care, and support for people out of work are under strain. Most arguments about them ask whether they are too big, too generous, or too expensive. This essay asks a different question. When conditions change, can these systems still correct course?This author’s answer is yes, but fewer of them can than most people assume, and the number falls each year they wait.The reason is not their size. It is that most of them have no working stopping rules. A stopping rule is a feedback loop that connects evidence of drift to corrective action in time. It gives a system a regular way to respond before a problem becomes much harder to fix. Where such rules do exist, they have often been allowed to wither, suspended the moment they began to bite.In time does not simply mean early. It means two things. First, the correction is still small enough to absorb. People can bear it without the system breaking the promises it exists to keep. Second, there is still enough time between the decision and its effect for people to plan around it. A worker told at 45 that her retirement age will rise by a year can adjust her savings and her plans. A worker told the same thing at 62 cannot. The same change is in time for one and too late for the other.When a correction is not made in time, its cost does not go away. It builds up. This essay calls that cost stewardship debt: the accumulated cost of corrections a state should have made and did not. Software engineers know a cousin of this idea as technical debt. Stewardship debt differs from the money a state borrows in one important way. Financial debt is counted every quarter. Stewardship debt appears on no balance sheet. It stays invisible until it comes due, and like any debt, it compounds.Left long enough, stewardship debt produces something worse. This essay calls it correction lock: the point at which an entity can no longer correct course, because any correction large enough to work now sets off the resistance, disruption, or loss of trust that stops it from being carried out. A small business reaches correction lock when it cannot cut costs without losing the people it needs to recover. A state reaches it when it cannot reform its pensions without bringing down its own government. At that point, the fix has become the thing that kills the fix.The consequences follow in a chain. Without correction in time, stewardship debt piles up. As it piles up, the state moves toward correction lock and loses its practical ability to govern. It can still see the problem, often in great detail. What it loses is the ability to agree on a response and carry it out. Citizens notice. They watch the same problems go unsolved year after year, and their confidence in the state falls. And that lost confidence makes the next correction harder still.That last step is a feedback loop too, but of the opposite kind. A stopping rule is a correcting loop: drift appears, and the system pulls it back. Distrust is a self-reinforcing loop: each failure makes the next failure more likely. Correction lock is the point where the second loop overpowers the first.This author calls the change in public confidence the move from assumed competence to inferred competence. When a state works, people assume it knows what it is doing. When it keeps failing to fix what everyone can see, they stop assuming and judge it by results instead. A state judged by its failures has little standing to ask anyone for sacrifice.France shows the whole chain at work. In the fall of 1995, Prime Minister Alain Juppé set out to cut the budget deficit ahead of the move to the euro, from about 5 percent of the economy to 3 percent. Part of his plan changed pensions for public-sector workers and for the special schemes that covered rail and transport staff. The workers walked out. Trains, subways, and buses stopped for weeks. Polls showed most of the public behind the strikers. On December 15, the government withdrew the pension changes. Other parts of the reform went ahead.Three decades later, France is having the same argument with far less room to move. Budget fights helped bring down Michel Barnier’s government in December 2024 and François Bayrou’s in September 2025. Public debt reached 119 percent of the economy in the second quarter of 2026. And the scheduled increases in retirement age and contribution years from the 2023 reform have been paused until 2028. France knows its problem. It has known it for thirty years. What it lacks is a way to settle it.Britain shows the same chain from another direction. France struggles to pass corrections. Britain has passed them and then not carried them out. In 2014, Parliament wrote a cap on the cost of care in old age into law. It was delayed, announced again, delayed again, and cancelled in 2024. It never took effect. Meanwhile, for fifteen years, Britain kept a pension rule that ran in only one direction. It could raise the state pension every year, but it could never slow it. A change was finally announced in 2026, to take effect in 2030.There are other good ways to look at all of this, and they overlap. Economic analysis looks at costs, revenues, and growth. Political analysis looks at consent, incentives, and institutions. The sociologist Wolfgang Streeck described borrowing as one way governments postponed the conflict between what markets demanded and what democracies had promised. He called it buying time. Demographic analysis looks at a population that is getting older. Each of these lenses is legitimate, and each points to its own kind of solution.This essay uses the lens of systems. It is not a better lens. It is a different one, and it leads to different solutions. Through it, a social model looks like any other system set on a course, whether a small business, a large company, or a nation. Conditions change. If the rules do not change with them, the system drifts. Drift by itself is not failure. Failure is drift that no feedback loop is set up to catch.Seen this way, a social model has two lines that matter most. One is what it has promised. The other is what it can pay for. When the lines run parallel, the system holds. When they start diverging, the gap grows a little each year. And as the gap grows, claims from different groups start converging on the same budget, leaving less room to satisfy any of them: young and old, workers and retirees, taxpayers and the people their taxes support.Illustrative, not data. The longer correction waits, the wider the gap and the harder the fix.A business that leaves that kind of gap alone eventually pays for it or fails. Failure is painful, but it ends. A state does not get that ending. A country can restructure its debts, but it cannot be wound up like a company. Its citizens still need hospitals, schools, pensions, and police the morning after. So stewardship debt does not disappear. It stays with the people, and it arrives as a choice among hard options: sharp cuts, higher taxes, a debt crisis, or a slow loss of faith in the state itself.There are a few things this essay does not argue. It does not forecast revolution in France, Britain, or anywhere else. It is not an argument against social protection, or against democracy. It takes no side between parties. Stewardship debt has been built up from more than one direction: by those who resist any change to benefits, and by those who cut taxes without cutting promises.So can Europe’s social model save itself? The rest of this essay argues that it can, but not through one more large reform. Large reforms are exactly what correction lock defeats. The way out is to close the feedback loop, with rules that act in time, while the changes are still small enough to bear. Sweden did it. Others built such rules and then switched them off. The question is whether the rest will build them, and keep them, before their stewardship debt makes the choice for them.Part twoThe warning was there from the start. In 1942, William Beveridge wrote the report that became the blueprint for Britain’s postwar social model. He saw that the population was aging and that birth rates were low, and he raised the need for later retirement. The people who designed these systems knew that their two lines would someday diverge.What they built was a large expansion of systems that already existed. Germany had introduced old-age pensions in 1889. After the Second World War, the rich democracies greatly widened pensions, health care, unemployment support, and public services. This author sees that expansion as part of the postwar settlement. Europe had just lived through two world wars, a depression, and movements that promised to tear the old order down. Social protection gave citizens a stake in the order that replaced it.For a time, the conditions were kind. Economies grew fast, and the working-age population was large. Promises were easy to finance. Then growth slowed, birth rates fell, and people lived longer. Governments changed benefits, contributions, and retirement ages. But the changes did not keep pace with the pressure.The size of that pressure is not in dispute. Pensions alone absorb about 14 percent of France’s economy, well above the OECD average of about 8 percent. In 2001, the European Union had almost four adults aged 20 to 64 for every person aged 65 or older. By 2020, it had fewer than three. Eurostat projects fewer than two by 2050. None of this is a secret. The projections have been public for decades. The question is what happens after the warning.Adults aged 20 to 64 for each person aged 65 or older, European Union. Source: Eurostat, Regional Yearbook 2021.]In France, the answer has been a long series of fights. After 1995 came a 2003 reform that raised the years of contributions required of public employees. A 2010 law began raising the retirement age from 60 to 62. A 2023 law scheduled a further rise to 64, and the government used Article 49.3 of the constitution to pass it without a final vote in the National Assembly. Now part of that law is on pause.Major French pension reforms since 1995.Each of these reforms improved the finances. None settled the long-term problem. The OECD’s 2026 review found that even the 2023 changes, carried out in full, would hold pension spending near 14 percent of the economy through 2045 while still leaving a funding shortfall. It again recommended tying the retirement age to life expectancy.France does not lack the first half of a feedback loop. It has advisory bodies, regular projections, and scheduled reviews. Its 2003 reform even tied the required years of contributions to life expectancy. What France lacks is the second half: a rule that turns a warning into a decision without a new political war each time. The signal is there. The loop is open. Every correction must be won from scratch, and each delay makes the next one larger, later, and harder to absorb. Each fight adds to the stewardship debt it was meant to pay down.Britain shows a different way to reach the same place. In 2011, it adopted the triple lock. Each year, the state pension rises by whichever is highest of inflation, average earnings, or 2.5 percent. It is a rule, but for fifteen years it ran in only one direction. It could raise pensions. It could never slow them. By the Institute for Fiscal Studies’ estimate, the triple lock cost about £16 billion more in 2026–27 than linking pensions to earnings would have. In its July 2026 projection, the Office for Budget Responsibility found it accounted for about half of the projected long-term rise in state pension spending. In September 2026, the government announced a change. From April 2030, the pension will still rise by at least inflation or 2.5 percent, but over time it will track average earnings rather than ratchet above them. Measured against this essay’s test, that change is in time: it is modest, it keeps a floor, and it gives years of notice. It took fifteen years to arrive.Long-term care tells a starker story. In 2011, a commission led by the economist Andrew Dilnot recommended a cap on what people pay for care in old age. Parliament wrote the cap into law in 2014. In 2015, it was delayed to 2020. In 2021, a new cap was announced for 2023. In 2022, it was delayed to 2025. In 2024, it was cancelled. A new commission is due to report in 2027. In fifteen years, no cap has ever taken effect. That is stewardship debt in plain sight: a problem everyone agrees exists, a fix written into law, and more than a decade of postponement that left the cost exactly where it was.France struggles to pass corrections. Britain has passed them and not carried them out. Other countries went further. They built the kind of rule this essay describes, then switched it off.By the OECD’s count in 2021, about two-thirds of its members had at least one automatic adjustment somewhere in their pension systems. On paper, that looks like the opposite of this essay’s claim. But look at what happened to those rules once they began to work.Germany created a sustainability factor in 2004. It slowed pension increases as the number of retirees grew relative to the number of workers paying in. Later, a guaranteed floor on pension levels overrode it. The 2025 pension package extended that suspension through 2031 and kept a guarantee that a standard pension would not fall below 48 percent of average earnings, measured before tax. The factor is due to return in 2032.Spain passed a sustainability factor in 2013. It was meant to adjust new pensions as life expectancy rose. It never took effect. It was postponed, then replaced in 2021.Outside Europe, Japan shows the same pattern. It introduced its own adjustment rule in 2004. Because of deflation and earlier benefit freezes, the rule was not applied even once until April 2015, eleven years later.That is what atrophy looks like. The loop is built, then cut. The rule is written. The rule is praised. Then, when it would actually cost someone something, it is suspended, delayed, or quietly replaced. And pensions are the best case. In this author’s reading, health care and long-term care, the costs that grow fastest as a society ages, are rarely governed by any automatic correction at all. So the record cuts two ways, and both support this essay’s claim. Outside pensions, stopping rules are mostly absent. Inside pensions, they are often switched off.Rules built to correct pensions and care costs, and what became of them.Sweden is the exception that shows what a working rule looks like. In the early 1990s, a severe economic crisis exposed the weaknesses of its old pension system. The government developed proposals in 1992. Parliament agreed on the main principles in 1994, passed most of the legislation in 1998, and added an automatic balancing rule in 2001. The parties behind the reform set up a joint group to oversee it together.The rule is simple to describe. When the income pension’s measured obligations grow larger than its assets, pensions and pension rights grow more slowly, or even fall. No new vote is needed. The rule works in time because it checks the balance every year and adjusts as soon as the system falls out of balance, while the gap is still small. After the 2008 financial crisis, it did exactly what it was built to do, and pensions fell in several years. It was painful. But the loop held, and nobody cut it.Sweden did not avoid a crisis. It reformed in the middle of one. What it did was make sure the next correction would not need another crisis to happen. That is the difference between a reform and a stopping rule. A reform pays down stewardship debt once. A stopping rule keeps it from piling up again.A reform pays down stewardship debt once. A stopping rule keeps it from piling up again.The cost of postponement reaches beyond the budget. When correction is absent or switched off, stewardship debt builds. Each year, governments have fewer changes they can agree on and carry out. Problems that could once have been handled through ordinary decisions turn into long struggles that bring down prime ministers. That is the road to correction lock. Part three follows it: how a state that can still see its problem loses the ability to act on it, and what happens when young and old start pulling on the same budget.Part threePostponement does not just delay a correction. It changes the correction. A change that could have been small and slow becomes large and fast.Go back to the worker from part one. Suppose a government decides to raise the retirement age by one year and announces it twenty years ahead. That asks very little of anyone. People in their forties adjust their plans. People near retirement are untouched. Now suppose the government waits fifteen years. The gap has grown, so the change must be bigger. The notice is shorter, so the change lands on people who have already made their plans. The same correction that once passed the test of in time now fails it on both counts. It is too large to absorb and too sudden to plan for.That is how stewardship debt turns into correction lock. The longer a state waits, the more its corrections look like the ones people resist hardest: large, abrupt, and aimed at those closest to the line. That resistance is not a sign that citizens fail to understand the problem. Often they understand it very well. A worker a few years from retirement who is suddenly told to work longer has a sound reason to object. The terms changed late, and that worker is the one paying for the delay. Sustained public opposition and strikes are what late corrections tend to produce.The second effect falls on the state itself. As stewardship debt grows, the set of changes a government can agree on and carry out gets smaller. Every option now costs someone a great deal. Coalitions that could have shared a small cost split over a large one. In France, governments have fallen over budgets, and the last major pension law could only pass by skipping a final vote in the National Assembly. That is a state that can still see its problem but can no longer act on it through ordinary decisions. Problems a healthy system would handle in a single budget turn into struggles that last for years. Those struggles consume the time and standing a government needs for everything else.The third effect falls on the public. Citizens watch the cycle. A reform is announced, fought over, delayed, diluted, or reversed. The problem remains. After enough rounds, people draw a conclusion about the state itself. They stop assuming it knows what it is doing. They start judging it by what it delivers. On this issue, what it has delivered is a decades-long argument with no settlement. That is the move from assumed competence to inferred competence.This is where the self-reinforcing loop takes over. Research by Julian Garritzmann, Erik Neimanns, and Marius Busemeyer, drawing on surveys across eight European countries, found that trust in government makes people more willing to accept reforms that carry costs and risks now in exchange for benefits later. Trust also softens the pull of narrow self-interest. Take trust away, and each person weighs a reform mainly by what it costs them personally. A state that has lost trust is asking people to accept a sacrifice on its word, and its word is exactly what it has spent. So the next correction gets harder. The delay grows, the correction needed grows with it, and the loop turns again.The fourth effect falls on the young. As the two lines diverge, claims converge on the same budget. Retirees, workers, students, and families all draw from it, and it cannot grow fast enough to meet every claim. The groups do not compete on equal terms. Older citizens vote at higher rates. Their benefits are protected by law, and sometimes by rules such as the triple lock Britain kept for fifteen years. The cost of delay falls hardest on those who arrive last.France makes the point with its own numbers. Its Pensions Advisory Council reports that French retirees today enjoy a standard of living roughly equal to that of the population as a whole, one of the highest levels among the countries the council tracks. That is a real achievement. But the council also projects that, by 2070, the relative standard of living of retirees will fall to around 90 percent of the population’s. Today’s young workers are paying into a system on terms that will not be offered to them when they retire.This is not a claim that the old are taking from the young on purpose. It is what happens when correction is postponed. Someone always pays for the delay. The people who pay are the ones who were not in the room when the decision was put off, and many of them were not yet old enough to vote.That is the full chain. A missing or switched-off feedback loop lets stewardship debt build. Stewardship debt makes each correction larger and later, which sets off the resistance that blocks it. Blocked corrections drain the state’s ability to govern. Citizens watch that failure and stop trusting the state. Lost trust makes the next correction harder still.At the end of the chain, a state still has every tool on paper. It can still pass laws, raise taxes, and change benefits. What it no longer has is the standing to use them. When that happens, change does not stop coming. It comes from outside the ordinary process. Part four looks at how that has happened before.Part fourWhen a state reaches correction lock, change does not stop. It moves. It leaves the ordinary process of budgets, laws, and elections and arrives some other way. History offers several versions of that move, and France has lived through more of them than most countries.The clearest case is the one this essay has already touched on. In 1786, the French monarchy was nearly bankrupt, largely from the cost of its wars. The finance minister, Charles Alexandre de Calonne, proposed what was in effect a stopping rule: a tax on land that the nobility and the clergy would pay like everyone else. He called an Assembly of Notables in 1787 to approve it. The notables refused. Calonne was dismissed. His successor tried again and was blocked by the courts. In August 1788, the king was forced to summon the Estates-General, which had not met since 1614. Days later, the treasury suspended payments.Look at that sequence through the chain in part three. The state could see its problem clearly. Its own ministers had written the fix. Each attempt to correct was blocked by those who would pay for it, and each failure made the next attempt larger and more urgent. By the end, the monarchy still had every tool on paper. It had lost the standing to use them. The correction came anyway, from outside the ordinary process. It came with the National Assembly of 1789 and the abolition of the old privileges that August.It is worth noticing who made that correction. It was not one group. The Third Estate held together lawyers, merchants, urban workers, and peasants, and they were joined by some liberal nobles and clergy. These people agreed on very little except that the old order could not go on. That was enough to bring it down. It was not enough to agree on what came next. Within five years, the revolution had turned on its own leaders, and the Terror settled the scores that the shared enemy had kept in check.The pattern repeats elsewhere. In Russia in February 1917, a broad and largely spontaneous coalition of workers, soldiers, liberals, and socialists brought down the tsar. In October, the Bolsheviks seized power from the government that February had created. They governed from December 1917 in coalition with the Left Socialist Revolutionaries, who gave them support among the peasants. That coalition broke in March 1918. By July, the Left Socialist Revolutionaries had risen up and been crushed. Allies for the seizure became enemies within months.Moments like these produce coalitions of convenience. Groups that share some grievances, but not all of them, set their differences aside to defeat a common target. They are often more effective together than they would be apart. The differences do not disappear. They wait. Once the old order falls, the fight over what replaces it begins, and the scores are settled then.Not every break looks like 1789 or 1917. Some are orderly. France’s Fourth Republic, created after the Second World War, went through more than twenty governments in twelve years. It could see its problems, above all the war in Algeria, but could not hold a government together long enough to resolve them. In 1958, after an army-backed uprising in Algeria, parliament gave Charles de Gaulle the power to write a new constitution. Voters approved it by a large majority. The Fifth Republic was born without a revolution in the streets. But the change still came from outside the ordinary process. The old system did not reform itself. It was replaced.Some breaks come from creditors. In 2010, Greece could no longer borrow on its own and accepted rescue loans on terms set largely by others. In 2011, as Italy’s borrowing costs soared, Silvio Berlusconi resigned, and a government led by the economist Mario Monti took office to push through changes that ordinary politics had not. In both cases, the decisions that elected governments had put off were made under pressure from markets and lenders. They were not made in time, and they were not made on terms the country chose.These cases differ in almost every way: monarchy and democracy, violence and peace, the eighteenth century and the twenty-first. What they share is the order of events. A problem was seen. Correction was postponed until it was too large to absorb. Ordinary decisions failed. Then change arrived from outside the process that was supposed to make it. The street, a new constitution, and the bond market are all versions of the same thing. Each is what correction looks like when the feedback loop has been open for too long.None of this is a forecast. France and Britain are not France in 1788, and nothing in this essay predicts a revolution, a new republic, or a debt crisis. In France today, parties on the far left and far right, who agree on little else, have voted together to bring down governments. That is a coalition of convenience at the level of parliament, not the street. Whether it ever becomes something more is unknowable. It is a reminder that the conditions for such coalitions are present.The point of this history is simpler. When a system reaches correction lock, it loses the ability to choose how it changes. The correction still happens. But its size, its speed, and who bears its cost are no longer decided by the system itself. They are decided by whoever and whatever is left standing. That is the real price of stewardship debt. It is not only that the bill gets larger. It is that someone else ends up deciding how it gets paid.Part five turns to the alternative: what it takes to keep that choice in a democracy’s own hands.Part fiveThe systems lens leads to a different kind of solution. It does not ask which policy is right: a higher retirement age, higher taxes, lower benefits, or some mix. Those are real choices, and economists and political leaders will keep arguing over them. The systems lens asks something earlier. Whatever a country decides, does it have a feedback loop that will correct that decision in time when conditions change? A good policy without such a loop will drift. A modest policy with one can be kept on course.Some readers will draw a different conclusion from this argument. If democracies cannot correct themselves, perhaps the answer is a government that does not need consent. In 2009, the columnist Thomas Friedman suggested that a one-party state, led by capable people, could have great advantages over democratic gridlock. The record supports a far more modest claim. A one-party state can start large projects quickly. It is not better at correcting drift. China kept its one-child policy for thirty-five years after the warnings began, and left retirement ages set in the 1950s in place for about seventy years. It held its zero-COVID policy for nearly three years, then dropped it almost overnight. And as part four showed, France’s own monarchy had near-absolute power before 1789 and still could not make the privileged pay. Alexis de Tocqueville observed that the most dangerous moment for a bad government is usually when it begins to reform. The economist Amartya Sen found that no major famine has struck a functioning democracy with a free press.The reason is the feedback loop. Rule without consent breaks it at the start, by silencing the warnings that show drift early: a free press, open data, and voters who complain. It can act, but it often cannot see. Democracies break the loop at the other end. They can see, but they struggle to act. The answer is not to give up seeing. It is to close the loop.A closed loop corrects drift. An open loop sees it but does not act. A broken signal can act but cannot see.What does a closed loop look like? Sweden’s pension rule is one example, but the parts are general. They apply to a pension system, a health service, a city budget, or a small business.First, a signal agreed in advance. The system names, ahead of time, what it will watch and what level counts as drift. In Sweden, it is the balance between the pension system’s obligations and its assets. Elsewhere it might be life expectancy, the ratio of workers to retirees, or the cost of care per person. The point is that the trigger is chosen before anyone knows whom it will hit. That is when agreement is easiest.Second, a response that happens by default. This is the part most systems leave out, and it is the part that matters most. In France, a warning leads to a debate. In Sweden, a warning leads to an adjustment, unless someone acts to stop it. That reverses the burden. Doing nothing no longer means drifting. It means correcting. A government that wants to suspend the rule must do so openly, and must explain who will pay instead.Third, corrections small enough to absorb. A loop that acts a little every year keeps each change in time. Denmark and the Netherlands both tie their retirement ages to life expectancy, so the age rises gradually, with years of notice, as people live longer. No single step is large enough to bring down a government.Fourth, a floor. A rule that only balances an account can still leave people with too little to live on. Sweden pairs its balancing rule with a guaranteed minimum pension, paid from taxes, for those with low lifetime earnings. A loop that protects the system’s finances while abandoning the people it exists to serve will not keep public support for long.Fifth, a review of the rule itself. Conditions change, and a rule set in 2001 may not suit 2040. A working loop includes a regular, scheduled point where the rule can be revised, in the open, by the same broad agreement that created it.There is a fair objection to all of this. A rule does not remove politics. It contains choices about who bears costs, and an elected government can always change it. Germany’s sustainability factor shows exactly that. And perhaps the deeper problem is not the missing rule but the disagreement that kept it from being built in the first place.The answer is that no rule can remove disagreement, and none should try. Disagreement is part of self-government. What a loop changes is the default. Without one, every correction must be won from scratch, against those who will pay for it, at the moment the cost is clearest. With one, correction happens unless someone stops it, and stopping it has to be done in the open. That does not guarantee good outcomes. Germany chose to suspend its rule, and that was a legitimate choice. But it was a visible one, with a date for the rule’s return. Rejecting a correction still leaves the problem in place. A loop makes sure that fact cannot be quietly forgotten.Sweden also answers the question of how such rules get built. Its parties did not agree on everything. They agreed, during a crisis, on a process. That is the lesson for any country that wants a loop: the time to build one is before the next correction is due, while no one yet knows whose turn it will be.Pensions are where this has been tried most. They are not where it is needed most. Health care and long-term care are growing faster as populations age, and, in this author’s reading, they are rarely governed by any automatic correction at all. Britain’s fifteen-year argument over a care cap is what a missing loop looks like in that field. The same principles apply there: an agreed signal, a default response, small steps, a floor, and a review.Leadership matters too, though less than people expect. Most campaigns promise large changes, and large changes are exactly what correction lock defeats. A candidate who runs on maintenance offers something less exciting: small, tolerable adjustments made every year to keep the lines parallel. It is not a promise that wins applause. But voters can reward it, and the countries that have kept their loops closed have usually had leaders willing to make it. Institutions matter more than any one leader, because a good loop keeps working after the leader who built it has gone.This principle does not belong to one party. It does not say whether a country should spend more or less, or tax more or less. A country with a large social model and a closed loop is in better shape than a country with a small one and an open loop. What the principle asks of every side is the same: decide what you are promising, decide how you will know when the promise is drifting, and decide in advance what will happen when it does.So, can Europe’s social model save itself? It can. Sweden shows that a democracy can build a loop, keep it running through a crisis, and accept the pain it brings without tearing the loop out. Germany, Spain, and others have shown that the loops can be built and then cut. France and Britain show what happens when the signal is clear and the loop stays open: stewardship debt builds, governments fall, trust drains away, and the choices narrow.The question is not whether Europe can afford its promises. That depends on choices each country must make for itself. The question is whether it will build the means to keep those promises in line with reality, and keep them running, before correction lock makes the choice for it. Every year of delay makes that harder. None of it is out of reach yet.ReferencesOn the 1995 strikes: Swarthmore Global Nonviolent Action Database, French public sectors strike against the Juppé Plan, 1995.On French public debt: INSEE, Informations Rapides, public debt at the end of the second quarter of 2026, 29 September 2026.On the 2023 reform, its pause, and the OECD assessment: OECD Economic Survey of France, 2026.On borrowing as buying time: Wolfgang Streeck, Buying Time: The Delayed Crisis of Democratic Capitalism, Verso, 2014.On the triple lock and its reform: Institute for Fiscal Studies, New Triple Lock Much Improved, Not Perfect, and Not Enough to Fund Universal Social Care, 29 September 2026; Office for Budget Responsibility, Fiscal Risks and Sustainability, July 2026; Department for Work and Pensions, State Pension Uprating Analysis 2026.On the care cap: Commission on Funding of Care and Support (Dilnot Commission), Fairer Care Funding, 2011; Care Act 2014.On Beveridge: William Beveridge, Social Insurance and Allied Services, HMSO, 1942.On pension spending: OECD, Pensions at a Glance.On the EU old-age dependency ratio: Eurostat, Regional Yearbook 2021.On automatic adjustment rules: OECD, Pensions at a Glance 2021, chapter on automatic adjustment mechanisms.On Germany: Rentenpaket 2025.On Spain: BBVA Research, A Decade of Pension Reforms and Counter-Reforms, 2021.On Japan: Japan Center for Economic Research, The Macroeconomic Slide Mechanism of the Japanese Pension System.On Sweden: Swedish Pensions Agency, Summary on the Swedish Pension System; WSI Study No. 28e, Politics and Organized Interests in Swedish Pension Policy, 2021.On trust and support for reform: Julian L. Garritzmann, Erik Neimanns, and Marius R. Busemeyer, “Public Opinion towards Welfare State Reform: The Role of Political Trust and Government Satisfaction,” European Journal of Political Research 62, no. 1 (2023): 197–220.On the standard of living of French retirees: Conseil d’orientation des retraites, Dossier en bref, February 2026; Rapport annuel, June 2026.On the fiscal crisis of the French monarchy: William Doyle, The Oxford History of the French Revolution, 3rd ed., Oxford University Press, 2018.On the Russian Revolution and the Left Socialist Revolutionaries: Orlando Figes, A People’s Tragedy: The Russian Revolution, 1891–1924, Jonathan Cape, 1996.On the end of the Fourth Republic: Julian Jackson, A Certain Idea of France: The Life of Charles de Gaulle, Allen Lane, 2018.On the European debt crisis: Adam Tooze, Crashed: How a Decade of Financial Crises Changed the World, Allen Lane, 2018.On China-envy: Thomas L. Friedman, “Our One-Party Democracy,” New York Times, September 8, 2009.On Tocqueville: Alexis de Tocqueville, The Old Regime and the Revolution, 1856.On famines and democracy: Amartya Sen, Development as Freedom, Knopf, 1999.On China’s retirement ages: National People’s Congress Standing Committee decision on raising the statutory retirement age, September 2024.On retirement ages tied to life expectancy: OECD, Pensions at a Glance 2023.John Elijah is a pen name. The writer behind it has spent more than twenty years inside large institutions, public and private, and holds a graduate degree in organization development. He writes on institutions, merit, and machines."}, {"title": "The Posting Has Outgrown the Job", "subtitle": "Every hire fills a hole. The posting has stopped describing it.", "topic": "Work & economy", "url": "/essays/the-posting-has-outgrown-the-job/", "text": "A small hole and a long list: the gap a hire is meant to fill, and the posting that was supposed to describe it.Every hire begins with a hole. Someone left, or the work grew past the people who were doing it. Before anyone writes a word, the hole has a shape. It is the work that is not getting done, and that work has edges.The last essay argued that companies have never described the person they want more exactly, or known less about whether they got one. This one is about an earlier step. Before a company can check whether it filled the hole, it has to describe the hole. That is the step that has quietly stopped working. The posting is meant to trace the shape of the gap. Past a certain line, it traces something else.Deep and wideGaps come in two shapes.A deep gap is one thing that has to be done well. The person who can read a packet capture and tell you what happened at two in the morning. The person who knows the old billing system because she built half of it. The actuary. The surgeon. The translator who knows the legal register of one language pair. Deep work is bought with hours spent on one thing, and the hours are not refundable. Nobody gets deep in six things, not because talent is scarce but because the week is.A wide gap is many things that have to be done well enough. The operations lead at a sixty-person company who does vendor contracts on Monday, a hiring plan on Tuesday, and an outage on Wednesday night. The one person in a ten-person firm who is finance, HR and facilities. The first product manager. Wide work is bought with the same hours spread thin, and the skill in it is knowing how thin is safe.Most jobs lean one way or the other. A few need both, and the people who hold them are the ones everyone names in the first ten minutes of a reorganization. Depth is not better than width. But the gap leans one way before the posting exists, and the posting is supposed to say which.Two ways to leave the gap behindPostings drift from the gap in both directions.The deep gap acquires width. The specialist who needs to do one thing well must also present to executives, run projects, mentor juniors, own vendor relationships, and be fluent in a list of tools long enough that two of them do the same job. The one thing the gap needs is still on the list. It is now line four of twenty.The wide gap acquires depth. The generalist who needs to do eight things adequately must be an expert in each of them. Eight experts’ worth of depth, in one person, at one salary. The posting is describing a department.The drift runs both ways partly because of where gaps come from.A gap left by attrition is person-shaped. Someone did the job for nine years, and the posting traces her whole outline: the thing she was hired for, the three things she grew into, the two things she did because nobody else would, and the one thing she was the last person in the building to know. The list is accurate. It is a biography. Nobody who has not spent nine years in that chair can match it, and the one person who could has just left.A gap opened by growth is guess-shaped. The work has not been done yet, so nobody knows its edges, and the honest answer to “what does this role need” is “we will find out.” Postings do not say that. They hedge. Everything that might be needed goes in, because leaving something out feels like a decision and putting it in feels like diligence.Two shapes of gap, and where the posting drifts. The deep gap is given width it does not need. The wide gap is given depth it cannot use.Then the committee arrives. Peter Cappelli, who has been saying this about hiring for years, describes the plain version: the manager decides what the job needs, then asks everyone who will work with the new person what they would like to see, and nobody whittles the answers down. They are added up. [1] In practice the adding has more hands than that. A peer adds the tools the team uses. A second peer adds the ones it is about to adopt. Recruiting adds the lines from the last posting with the same title, which inherited them from the one before. Legal adds a paragraph. Nobody is paid to subtract, and nobody is blamed for a requirement that was never needed. The list grows the way a hallway fills with boxes: one at a time, each one somebody’s good reason.And then there is the market. The one piece of outside evidence this author went looking for holds, and it holds in both directions. When more people are looking for work, companies ask for more from the same title at the same company. [2] The published estimate is that the rise in job seekers during the recession accounts for 18 to 25 percent of the rise in posted requirements between 2007 and 2010. [2] When the market tightened again after 2010, the same researchers watched the requirements come back down. [3] The title had not changed. The list had. The list is partly a thermometer. It measures how many people are standing outside the door.The cleanest specimen of a list leaving the work behind is a few years old now. A programmer saw a posting that required four years’ experience with a software framework. He could not apply. The framework was about a year and a half old. He knew, because he had written it. [4] The same week, a posting at one of the largest technology companies in the world asked for twelve years’ experience with a tool that had existed for six. [4] Nobody could have met those lines, which means they were never descriptions of a gap. They were a habit: a number added to a noun.What the twentieth line doesHere is the part this author thinks is new, or at least not said plainly enough.The lines that describe the gap do real work. They separate people who can do the job from people who cannot. Each one carries information, and the company learns something about every candidate who passes it.Past the shape of the gap, the lines stop carrying information and start selecting for something else. A twenty-item list has a property a five-item list does not: almost nobody meets all of it well. So the list is no longer asking who can do this. It is asking who will say they can.Candidates read a list like that in one of two ways. The literal reader counts. She can do twelve of the twenty well, three passably, and has never touched the other five. She takes the list at its word and does not apply. The rounding reader also counts, reaches the same twelve, and rounds the rest up. Familiar with becomes experienced in. Two years becomes four. A tool he watched a colleague use becomes a tool he knows.A requirement the work does not need cannot select for the work. There is nothing in the work for it to select. What it selects for is the willingness to claim it. That is the whole argument in one sentence. Every line past the shape of the gap costs the company a literal reader and keeps a rounding one. And the literal readers are not a random slice of the pool. They are the careful ones, and for a deep gap, care is the job.A requirement the work does not need cannot select for the work. What it selects for is the willingness to claim it.Opinion 1. This is why this author calls the returns negative, not merely diminishing. A line that adds nothing and costs nothing would be harmless clutter. A line that adds nothing and costs the careful candidates is a filter working against the company that wrote it.This author has looked for a study that tests this and has not found one. The absence deserves a sentence of its own. Companies have been lengthening these lists for at least fifteen years, and nobody has measured what the length does to who applies. The last essay said why: the measures that survive are the ones that can be found quickly, move this quarter, and read at a glance. This one fails all three. What can be found is thinner, and here it is.In a British experiment with more than ten thousand current and prospective job seekers, the point at which people said they would apply came, on the researchers’ estimate, at a little over half the listed requirements, for men and women alike. [5] The familiar claim that women wait until they meet all of them while men go at sixty percent turns out to have no data behind it, and a second study in 2024 found no reliable gender gap either. [5] [6] The list in that experiment had five items. People said they would go at about three. What they do at twenty, nobody has measured, and the claim above should be read with that in mind.In a 2025 survey of two thousand American job seekers, a quarter admitted lying on a resume, and the two most common lies were years of experience and skills. [7] The survey did not ask what the postings looked like. It says only where people round, and they round in the two currencies a posting is written in: years and skills. And in a study of a million and a half Austrian postings, the ones that listed more skills stayed open a little longer. [8]Put that together and this author’s claim is this: the people who answer a twenty-item posting sort, with few exceptions, into two groups. Those who can do some of it well, and those who say they can do all of it. The company then interviews a pool it has tilted toward the second group, with a process that, as the last essay argued, cannot check. [9]What the gap would sayOpinion 2. If the gap could write its own posting, it would be short, and it would have a shape.A deep gap would ask for the one thing, at depth, and say how depth will be checked: a problem to work, a system to read, an hour with the people who will depend on the answer. It would not ask the specialist to also be a presenter, a project manager and a mentor, because every one of those lines sends away someone who spent the hours on the one thing instead.A wide gap would ask for range and say so. Eight areas, competence in most, strength in two, and the judgment to know which is which this week. It would not say expert eight times, because that word is a filter for people who will say it back.Opinion 3. Neither would be hard to write. What is hard is the subtraction. Someone has to strike the peer’s favorite tool, the departed employee’s ninth year, recruiting’s inherited paragraph, and the number that got added to the noun. Nobody is rewarded for that. Nobody is blamed for its absence. So the list grows, the gap keeps its own shape underneath, and the two drift apart a line at a time.The last essay ended with the one part of hiring nobody designed, the referral, and the odd fact that it keeps producing hires out of all proportion to the applications it brings in. Add one more reason. At its best, the person who refers a candidate knows two shapes: the shape of the gap, because she works next to it, and the shape of the candidate, because she worked next to him. She is not matching a list. She is matching a hole to a person. That is what the posting was for, before it started describing the market, the committee, and the person who left.ReferencesPeter Cappelli, “Your Approach to Hiring Is All Wrong,” Harvard Business Review, May–June 2019, https://hbr.org/2019/05/your-approach-to-hiring-is-all-wrong. The account paraphrased here is from his Knowledge at Wharton interview, October 4, 2019, https://knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu/podcast/knowledge-at-wharton-podcast/fix-the-hiring-process/Alicia Sasser Modestino, Daniel Shoag and Joshua Ballance, “Upskilling: Do Employers Demand Greater Skill When Workers Are Plentiful?”, Review of Economics and Statistics 102, no. 4 (2020): 793–805, https://doi.org/10.1162/rest_a_00835Alicia Sasser Modestino, Daniel Shoag and Joshua Ballance, “Downskilling: Changes in Employer Skill Requirements over the Business Cycle,” Harvard Kennedy School Faculty Research Working Paper RWP16-014, March 2016, https://www.hks.harvard.edu/publications/downskilling-changes-employer-skill-requirements-over-business-cycle“IBM job ad calls for 12 years’ experience with Kubernetes, which is six years old,” The Register, July 13, 2020, https://www.theregister.com/2020/07/13/ibm_kubernetes_experience_job_ad/, which reproduces Sebastián Ramírez’s post of July 11, 2020 on the FastAPI posting. FastAPI’s first public release, version 0.1.0, is dated December 8, 2018: https://pypi.org/project/fastapi/0.1.0/Leonie Nicks, Filip Gesiarz, Lourdes Valencia, Tim Hardy and Johannes Lohmann, “Gender Differences in Response to Requirements in Job Adverts,” Behavioural Insights Team with the Government Equalities Office, March 2022, https://www.bi.team/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Gender-differences-in-response-to-requirements-in-job-adverts-March-2022.pdfMona Salwender and Dagmar Stahlberg, “Do Women Only Apply When They Are 100% Qualified, Whereas Men Already Apply When They Are 60% Qualified?”, European Journal of Social Psychology 54, no. 7 (2024): 1545–1557, https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.3109ResumeBuilder.com, “1 in 4 Americans Have Lied on Their Resume, and Many Say It Helped Their Careers,” survey of 2,000 U.S. job applicants conducted by Pollfish, January 2025, https://www.resumebuilder.com/resume-examples/1-4-americans-have-lied-on-their-resume/Lennart Ziegler, “Skill Demand and Wages: Evidence from Linked Vacancy Data,” IZA Discussion Paper 14511, June 2021, https://docs.iza.org/dp14511.pdfJohn Elijah, “The Hiring Blind Spot,” Substack, October 2026, https://johnelijah1.substack.com/p/the-hiring-blind-spotFacts are sourced. Opinions are marked."}, {"title": "The Hiring Blind Spot", "subtitle": "The more exactly a company describes the person it wants, the less it can tell whether it got one. The only part of hiring that still knows was never written down.", "topic": "Work & economy", "url": "/essays/the-hiring-blind-spot/", "text": "Twenty qualifications, twenty-two named tools, and the job never uses two of them.Postings of a certain kind are everywhere now. One, for an analyst at a security company, asks for a single person who can read raw network traffic and brief an executive, write code and run a server, follow a foreign adversary and lead a team, and then stand up at a conference and talk about all of it. Twenty qualifications. Twenty-two named tools, formats, and protocols. Pieces of at least five occupations, by the government’s classification. Two of the skills it demands appear nowhere in the work it describes. None of this is unusual. The average posting now names about 14 skills, and postings in software and project management name more than 20. [1]Here is the strange part. A year after that job is filled, nobody at that company will be able to say which of the twenty mattered. Not because the question is hard, but because no one will have asked it. In a 2025 survey of about 1,300 senior recruiting professionals, one in four said they were highly confident their organization could measure quality of hire. [2] The other three will know, to the day, how long the position took to fill and what it cost.Opinion 1. That is the condition of hiring now. The description of the person has become precise, the knowledge of whether the hire worked has disappeared, and the two facts are connected. The precision is the cause of the blindness.The precision is the cause of the blindness.The reason is a rule about what organizations measure. A company measures what it can find, what moves this quarter, and what reads at a glance. Governance, Bureaucracy, and Organization makes that argument about administrative systems in general. Everything that passes those tests gets managed. Everything that fails them gets a proxy, and the proxy gets managed instead. A requirement is a field in a database: it can be found, it fills a role this quarter, and it reads in a second. Whether the hire worked is none of those things. It arrives a year later, spread across one manager’s judgment and a few colleagues’ willingness to say so, and it comes as a story rather than a number. So the posting gets more exact every year and the result gets no clearer, and the first is why the second never improves: every item added to the list is one more thing the company would have to check and never will. Peter Cappelli, who has been saying this for years, puts it plainly: firms manage the speed and cost of hiring because those are the numbers they have. [3]Three tests a measure has to pass to stay on the chart. The first three rows are on every recruiting dashboard. The fourth is not.The four designed parts of hiring show the rule at work, in order. The fifth part, the one that still knows whether the hire worked, was never designed at all.The nameThe job title is the most precise thing in the posting and the least informative. It is a field in a database, so it is counted, searched, and compared in seconds. The federal government recognizes 867 detailed occupations, up from 821 in 2000. [4] One company that collects postings starts from millions of raw titles and boils them down to about 75,000 standardized ones. [5] That is 86 names for every occupation. Some of those names are real: one study traced when each title in the Census index first appeared and found that about 60 percent of 2018 employment sits in specialties that did not exist in 1940. [6] Some are not: in one profession with good records, the same nursing role carried a wide range of titles, and the title chosen tracked the pay band. [7] Nobody can say the split, because a title is checked against nothing. It is written once, at the top of the posting, for the search engine, and it is never compared with the work the person went on to do. A name that is never checked is free to multiply, and it has.The listBelow the name comes the list, and the list is where precision does its damage. Applying for a job became nearly free: a click, a stored resume, now a generated one. At one applicant-tracking company, inbound applications roughly tripled between 2021 and the start of 2024. [8] Employers had to cut the pile somehow, and the cheapest knife is a requirement. Require a degree. Require five years. Name the tool. Add the certification. Each item is reasonable on its own, each shrinks the pile, and none is ever removed, because nothing downstream ever reports that it was useless. After enough rounds the posting describes someone who can do twenty things.Opinion 2. Some of those twenty are real. About one posting in eight asks for a combination of skills that used to belong to different jobs, those jobs pay 20 to 40 percent more, and the firms that ask for technical and social skill in the same person are the better-paying ones. [9] [10] But a specialized job and a badly scoped one look the same on paper, and the only way to tell them apart, scoring the hire against the list a year later, is not practical at twenty items. So the list is never scored. It cannot be. The company can know the employee is doing well and still not know whether it hired for the right reasons, which is the one thing the list was written to establish.The evidence that lists describe wishes rather than jobs is not subtle. In 2017, researchers compared 26 million postings with the people already doing the work. For production supervisors, 67 percent of postings asked for a bachelor’s degree. Sixteen percent of working production supervisors had one. [11] Then came the test. Between 2014 and 2023, tens of thousands of roles at large employers dropped the degree requirement, usually with an announcement. In 2024 the same researchers checked the hires. In the roles that dropped it, non-degree hiring rose by 3.5 percentage points; across the economy, by 0.14 percent, less than one hire in 700. [12] The posting changed and the hiring did not, because the posting had never been connected to the decision in the first place. The minority of firms that changed their process rather than their posting raised non-degree hiring by about 18 percent, and those hires stayed longer than their degree-holding colleagues. [12] Where someone looked at the outcome, the outcome moved. Almost no one looked.The lists move with the market, not with evidence. The share of postings requiring a degree fell from 2019 to early 2024 and has been rising since the market cooled; the share naming years of experience fell overall while in technology the share asking for five or more years rose. [13] [14] A list that lengthens when applicants are plentiful and shortens when they are scarce is measuring the employer’s leverage, which is visible this quarter, not the job, which is not. And the cost of the filter never appears on a report. Employers themselves say that rigid descriptions and automated screening reject people who could do the work. [15] Those people never become employees whose performance could be measured. They vanish from the evidence. The employer has solved a measurable problem, too many applications, by taking on an unmeasured one.The conversationThe interview should be the correction. It is the one point where a human could set the list aside and ask what the job is and whether this person can do it. The research on which interviews work is old, large, and settled: the structured kind, the same job-related questions for everyone and a scoring guide, predicts performance about as well as anything the field has, and the unstructured conversation predicts it much worse. A 2022 reanalysis lowered every estimate and kept the order; the structured interview came out on top. [16] [17] The field has known for a generation.Most candidates get the conversation anyway, and the measurement rule explains why. A conversation produces an impression, formed in an hour and held with confidence, and an impression reads at a glance. A structured interview produces a score sheet that has to be designed, which requires first deciding what the job is, which is the work the twenty-item list was written to avoid. In a 2013 set of experiments, people who ran interviews built from deliberately random questions still formed confident judgments, those judgments made their predictions worse, and when offered the choice they preferred a random interview to none. [18] Feeling informed is not being informed, and nothing afterward compares the feeling to the hire. Even a structured interview cannot repair an undefined job. Pointed at twenty requirements, it only evaluates them more consistently. The process gets more disciplined without getting more accurate.The numbers that fill the holeWhat happens after the hire should be where the company finds out. Instead it is where the emptiness becomes visible, because the slot where the outcome belongs is filled with figures that sound like outcomes. A third of new hires leave within 90 days. Nearly half fail within 18 months. A bad hire costs 30 percent of first-year salary, according to the Department of Labor. Every one of those is a proxy wearing a number. The first is a 2018 vendor survey in which 30 percent of respondents said they had, at some point in their careers, left a job within its first 90 days. [19] The second is a 2005 poll of managers in which failure included a poor review. [20] The third has no traceable source at the Department of Labor or anywhere else. [21] The hiring industry’s statistics about its own failures were never measured either. They circulate because they read at a glance and nothing better exists to contradict them.The government data that do exist point the other way and still cannot answer the question. Median tenure with the current employer was 4.1 years in January 2026, up from 3.9 two years before, and the share of workers with a year or less of tenure fell. [22] About five million people are hired every month and about five million leave, and no public table says how long the leavers had been there. [23] Whether a hire worked is not a number anyone collects, inside companies or outside them.One signal comes through, and it comes as a story, from the people who left. In every survey that asks, the leading reason for leaving a job early is that the work was not what the person was told: 43 percent of early leavers in 2018, and in 2025, 36 percent of respondents who had left a job within 90 days over the gap between the interview and the role. [19] [24] The remedy is known and old. Telling candidates the unattractive parts of a job before they take it lowers early turnover by a small, repeatable amount across roughly fifty studies. [25] [26] It works by giving both sides the information the twenty-item list did not contain. Some postings never meant to contain it. One hiring platform found that in a typical quarter about a fifth of the jobs on its system were listings with no active effort to fill them, and that seven in ten of its customers had posted at least one. [27] A posting counts as activity whether or not anyone is hired from it. That is the measurement rule at its most literal.The part nobody designedWhich leaves the referral, and the referral fails every test the rest of hiring passes. It cannot be located; it happens in a conversation before the company sees the applicant. It does not appear on this quarter’s report except as a bonus paid. It cannot be read at a glance, because it is one person’s judgment of another person’s work, delivered late and in words. It is exactly the kind of soft measure that drops off the chart. It is also the only part of hiring with a record of working.At the applicant-tracking company whose inbound pile tripled, referrals were about one percent of applications and 17 to 18 percent of hires, and referred candidates reached interviews and offers at far higher rates than anyone who came in through the front door. [8] Older benchmarks put referrals at a quarter to a third of hires. [28] So this is not how most people get jobs; four hires in five come some other way. It is the one channel whose results differ in kind. The best study of why used personnel records from nine large firms. In the lower-skill firms, referred employees were about 10 to 11 percent less likely to leave. They were not consistently more productive. [29] A study inside a single firm’s referral system found the same, and so did a study that followed 1,700 applicants to one phone service center: referred candidates advantaged at every stage, from interview to offer to retention, because the employees who referred them had screened and informed them before the company saw a resume. [30] [31]Referrals at one applicant-tracking company, 2021 to mid-2025: about one percent of applications and 17 to 18 percent of hires.Opinion 3. Better retention without better productivity is what information looks like. The referral does not find a better worker. It finds a better match, because it is the only step at which two things happen that nothing else provides: someone who has seen the candidate’s work vouches for it, and someone who knows the job tells the candidate what it is. Those are the two facts the twenty-item list was supposed to carry and cannot. The volume of the last few years sharpened the contrast without changing it: applications tripled, the referral share stayed flat, and the gap between the two channels in how often an application became a job widened. [8] The formal channel gained volume, which goes on the dashboard. It did not gain information, which does not.The referral is not clean. Networks reproduce themselves. One firm’s hiring records showed employee networks carrying existing racial disparities into hiring without anyone preferring anyone, and a study of Brazilian employer records found both things at once: referred hires dismissed less often, and the makeup of the networks passing inequality forward. [32] [33] A channel that runs through friends corrects for fit and not for fairness, and neither result shows up on the chart either.What the hire costsOpinion 4. Hiring is one instance. The same three tests govern whatever a company knows about itself, and they fail the same way for anything whose result arrives late, spread across people, and in words: whether training took, whether the reorganization held, whether the people who know how the place works are still in it. What passes the tests gets managed. What fails them gets a proxy, and the proxy gets managed instead, until the proxy is the only thing anyone can see. Companies have become very good at filling jobs quickly and cheaply, because that is what they can see, and they have become more exact about the person they want at the same rate, because exactness is cheap to write. Neither of those tells them whether the person they got was the person they needed. A company knows the cost of filling a position to the dollar. It does not know the cost of defining the position badly, and the more precisely it defines it, the less able it is to find out.Opinion 5. The referral survived all of this because it carries the one vital sign the chart cannot, in the only form that sign comes in: one person’s judgment of another person’s work. Nobody designed it. Nobody could have. It is the part of hiring that still measures the thing the rest of hiring stopped looking at.ReferencesIndeed Hiring Lab, “How Skill Concentration Sets Tech and Healthcare Sectors Apart,” June 2026. https://www.hiringlab.org/2026/06/03/how-skill-concentration-sets-tech-and-healthcare-sectors-apart/LinkedIn Talent Solutions, The Future of Recruiting 2025. https://business.linkedin.com/hire/resources/future-of-recruitingPeter Cappelli, “Your Approach to Hiring Is All Wrong,” Harvard Business Review, May–June 2019. https://hbr.org/2019/05/your-approach-to-hiring-is-all-wrongOffice of Management and Budget, 2018 Standard Occupational Classification, Federal Register notice, July 22, 2016. https://www.bls.gov/soc/2018/frn_july_22_2016.pdfLightcast, Titles Taxonomy. https://lightcast.io/taxonomies/titles-taxonomyDavid Autor, Caroline Chin, Anna Salomons, and Bryan Seegmiller, “New Frontiers: The Origins and Content of New Work, 1940–2018,” Quarterly Journal of Economics 139, no. 3 (2024). https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-12/New%20Frontiers%20-%20The%20Origins%20and%20Content%20of%20New%20Work%201940-2018.pdfAlison Leary and co-authors, “Variation in Job Titles within the Nursing Workforce,” Journal of Clinical Nursing, 2017. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28880423/Ashby, Talent Trends: “Are Referred Candidates More Likely to Get Hired?” 2024–2025. https://www.ashbyhq.com/talent-trends-report/reports/referralsBurning Glass Technologies, The Hybrid Job Economy, 2019, as summarized at https://www.td.org/content/atd-blog/hybrid-jobs-need-hybrid-skillsDavid Deming and Lisa Kahn, “Skill Requirements across Firms and Labor Markets: Evidence from Job Postings for Professionals,” Journal of Labor Economics 36, no. S1 (2018). https://econpapers.repec.org/RePEc:ucp:jlabec:doi:10.1086/694106Joseph Fuller and Manjari Raman, Dismissed by Degrees, Harvard Business School, Accenture, and Grads of Life, 2017. https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/dismissed-by-degrees_707b3f0e-a772-40b7-8f77-aed4a16016cc.pdfJoseph Fuller, Christina Langer, and Matt Sigelman, Skills-Based Hiring: The Long Road from Pronouncements to Practice, Burning Glass Institute and Harvard Business School, 2024. https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/Skills-Based%20Hiring_80b90afa-f6b5-4de9-b7f8-5d8232ce20e2.pdfIndeed Hiring Lab, “Educational Requirements Are Gradually Disappearing from Job Postings,” February 2024, https://www.hiringlab.org/2024/02/27/educational-requirements-job-postings/; and “Where Do College Degrees Still Matter in a Skills-First Job Market?” January 2026, https://www.hiringlab.org/2026/01/28/where-do-college-degrees-still-matter-in-a-skills-first-job-market/Indeed Hiring Lab, 2025 US Jobs and Hiring Trends Report, December 2024, https://www.hiringlab.org/2024/12/10/indeed-2025-us-jobs-and-hiring-trends-report/; and “Experience Requirements Have Tightened Amid the Tech Hiring Freeze,” July 2025, https://www.hiringlab.org/2025/07/30/experience-requirements-have-tightened-amid-the-tech-hiring-freeze/Joseph Fuller, Manjari Raman, and co-authors, Hidden Workers: Untapped Talent, Harvard Business School and Accenture, 2021. https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/hiddenworkers09032021_Fuller_white_paper_33a2047f-41dd-47b1-9a8d-bd08cf3bfa94.pdfFrank Schmidt and John Hunter, “The Validity and Utility of Selection Methods in Personnel Psychology,” Psychological Bulletin 124, no. 2 (1998): 262–274.Paul Sackett, Charlene Zhang, Christopher Berry, and Filip Lievens, “Revisiting Meta-Analytic Estimates of Validity in Personnel Selection,” Journal of Applied Psychology 107, no. 11 (2022): 2040–2068. Summary at https://www.siop.org/tip-article/is-cognitive-ability-the-best-predictor-of-job-performance-new-research-says-its-time-to-think-again/Jason Dana, Robyn Dawes, and Nathaniel Peterson, “Belief in the Unstructured Interview: The Persistence of an Illusion,” Judgment and Decision Making 8, no. 5 (2013): 512–520. https://ideas.repec.org/a/cup/judgdm/v8y2013i5p512-520_1.htmlJobvite, Job Seeker Nation Survey, 2018. https://web.jobvite.com/rs/328-BQS-080/images/2023-01-2018JobSeekerNationSurvey.pdfLeadership IQ, Why New Hires Fail, 2005. https://www.leadershipiq.com/blogs/leadershipiq/35353729-why-new-hires-fail-emotional-intelligence-vs-skillsOn the untraceable Department of Labor figure, see the source-tracing at https://www.frontlinesourcegroup.com/blog-us-department-of-labor-bad-hire-30-percent-of-first-year-earnings.htmlBureau of Labor Statistics, Employee Tenure in 2026. https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/tenure.pdfBureau of Labor Statistics, Job Openings and Labor Turnover Summary, August 2026. https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.nr0.htmEmploy, 2025 Job Seeker Nation Report. https://pages.employinc.com/rs/659-JST-226/images/2025-Job-Seeker-Nation-Report.pdfJean Phillips, “Effects of Realistic Job Previews on Multiple Organizational Outcomes: A Meta-Analysis,” Academy of Management Journal 41, no. 6 (1998): 673–690. Summary at https://www.qic-wd.org/umbrella-summary/realistic-job-previewsDavid Earnest, David Allen, and Ronald Landis, “Mechanisms Linking Realistic Job Previews with Turnover: A Meta-Analytic Path Analysis,” Personnel Psychology 64, no. 4 (2011): 865–897. https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1744-6570.2011.01230.xGreenhouse, State of Job Hunting, 2024. https://www.greenhouse.com/blog/greenhouse-2024-state-of-job-hunting-reportSilkRoad and CareerXroads source-of-hire benchmarks, as reported at https://www.ere.net/articles/job-boards-referrals-lead-as-hiring-sources-agencies-yield-strong-hire-ratioStephen Burks, Bo Cowgill, Mitchell Hoffman, and Michael Housman, “The Value of Hiring through Employee Referrals,” Quarterly Journal of Economics 130, no. 2 (2015): 805–839. Summary at https://wol.iza.org/articles/the-value-of-hiring-through-employee-referrals-in-developed-countries/longMeta Brown, Elizabeth Setren, and Giorgio Topa, “Do Informal Referrals Lead to Better Matches? Evidence from a Firm’s Employee Referral System,” Journal of Labor Economics 34, no. 1 (2016): 161–209.Roberto Fernandez, Emilio Castilla, and Paul Moore, “Social Capital at Work: Networks and Employment at a Phone Center,” American Journal of Sociology 105, no. 5 (2000): 1288–1356.Roberto Fernandez and Isabel Fernandez-Mateo, “Networks, Race, and Hiring,” American Sociological Review 71, no. 1 (2006): 42–71. https://cpi.stanford.edu/_media/pdf/key_issues/social_networks_research.pdfConrad Miller and Ian Schmutte, “The Dynamics of Referral Hiring and Racial Inequality: Evidence from Brazil,” 2021. https://irle.berkeley.edu/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/The-Dynamics-of-Referral-Hiring-and-Racial-Inequality-Evidence-from-Brazil-1.pdfFacts are sourced. Opinions are marked."}, {"title": "Trust in AI Is Checked Now, Not Assumed", "subtitle": "Users, labs, and lawmakers each have to check whether the others can be trusted. Anthropic’s new usage policy shows all three doing it at once.", "topic": "Technology & society", "url": "/essays/trust-in-ai-is-checked-now-not-assumed/", "text": "The sentence that changed. Anthropic’s usage policy, effective November 12, 2026.The last essay by this author ended on one word: inferred. [21] Americans no longer assume that the technology companies can be trusted. They infer it, from what the companies do, and the inference is fraying. Governance, Bureaucracy, and Organization draws the same line between competence that is assumed and competence that has to be inferred. Trust works the same way. Anthropic’s new usage policy, which takes effect on November 12, is a good place to watch that inference being made, because it is being made at three levels at once. [1] At the first level, a company decides whether to trust the people who use its tool. At the second, those people, and everyone else, decide whether to trust the company. At the third, governments decide how far to trust either one and how soon to write rules. Each level has a different worry and needs a different answer. The policy, read beside the threat report Anthropic published in September, shows all three.The individualAt the first level, the company is the one inferring. It decides whether to trust the people who use its tool, and the evidence is what they do with it, watched by software. The policy says so in a sentence that changed. The version in force since September 2025 said that if the company learned you had violated it, it could throttle, suspend, or terminate your access. [2] The new one says that if the company suspects you may have violated it, it may warn, throttle, limit, suspend, or terminate you. [1] Learned became suspects. Had became may have.Before treating that as a scandal, consider the fair reading. Enforcement at this scale is done by software. A classifier does not produce findings. It produces suspicion. The old sentence described a standard the company probably never met in practice. The new sentence describes the one it actually uses. So the change is not a new power. It is an honest label on an old one. It is also what inferred trust looks like when a company does it to its users: nobody is assumed to be in good faith, and everyone is judged, continuously, by what they do.Whether that judgment is warranted is the question the casebook answers. On September 10, Anthropic published a threat report covering misuse of its models from December 2025 through August 2026, across seven areas: cyber operations, influence operations, surveillance, scams and fraud, biological misuse, conventional weapons development, and distillation. [3] The cases are specific. Russian actors, whom Anthropic says were not linked to the state, tried to use Claude to write software for an autonomous kamikaze drone swarm trained on Ukrainian combat footage. A consultant believed to work for Mali’s intelligence service used it to build a system for monitoring about 25 million phones and generating dossiers on targets. [4] The report counts six conventional weapons cases and five involving biology, and it says Chinese AI developers were accused of routing live customer conversations through Claude to harvest training data. [5] In the weapons cases, Anthropic says the actors used its coding tool, Claude Code, in place of software engineers to write guidance and control code. [6]Put the report beside the new policy and the match is close to line by line. The old weapons rule banned designing or producing weapons. The new one names drones, autonomous platforms, and software for targeting, fire control, and engagement. The old surveillance rule banned tracking a person’s location or communications without consent. The new one adds online activity, associations, analysis of data already collected, and the building of dossiers. There is a new ban on routing requests through consumer subscriptions or misrepresenting which product is being used, and a sharper one on using outputs to train other models. There is an entire new section on fake personas, hidden sponsorship, and networks of sites built to fool search engines and AI systems. [1] None of this is a guess about what people might do. It is a record of what they did, and the rules arrived about a month after the company described the cases.Opinion 1. That is the part this author finds most telling. It confirms what many people already suspected about AI: that it would be pulled toward weapons, surveillance, fraud, and influence operations as fast as toward anything else. The confirmation is not pleasant. It is still better to have it in writing from the company that watched it happen than to guess. And it settles the first level. A company that has seen this is right to infer trust in its users rather than assume it. The question is what everyone else can infer about the company.The companyAt the second level, the direction reverses. Users, and everyone else, infer whether to trust the company, and the evidence is what the company publishes and what it lets people check. The usual word for what this level needs is transparency. The word has been used so much that it has stopped meaning anything. Companies get credit for saying it, and nobody tests it. This essay uses a narrower test instead: can a person outside the company check it? A published casebook passes. A written standard passes, even an unflattering one. A reason and a date for a suspension would pass. A notice that says a user is being slowed down would pass. “We take this seriously” does not. By that test, Anthropic passes on two of the four things that matter and fails on the two that touch the user.The two passes are the casebook and the standard. The threat report is a published record of what the company caught, and the suspicion sentence is an accurate description of how it enforces, written in a way that reads worse than the polite version would have. Both can be checked. Both earn credit, and the second one cost the company something to say. The company that writes the accurate sentence invites criticism that the company keeping the polite one avoids.The two failures are what happens to a user on the other end of that suspicion. The first is the loud penalty, suspension. There is an appeal process. It lives in a support article, not in the policy. If you believe your account was wrongly suspended, you log in to the suspended account, fill out a form, and the safety team can investigate why the account was disabled. The company promises a reply as soon as it can and asks for patience. [7] There is no timeline. There is no commitment to tell you what you did. The form can only be reached by logging in to the account that was just suspended. [8] Users posting bug reports this summer described suspension emails that cited “suspicious signals” with no specific reason, and an appeal link that led back to the login page with no form behind it. [9] Those are user reports, not verified findings, and this essay cites them as such. But they describe exactly what a suspicion-based system would be expected to produce: a notice without a reason, and a door that may or may not open.This is not an Anthropic problem. It is an industry pattern, and the industry has already shown where it leads. In 2021 a San Francisco father photographed his toddler’s infection at a nurse’s request for a telehealth visit. Google’s automated scanning flagged the images as child abuse material, disabled his account, and reported him to the authorities. The police investigated and found no crime. Google never restored the account. [10] Everything in that story was done by a system acting on suspicion, and the one human decision in it, the refusal to reinstate, was the only one that could not be appealed.The second failure is the quiet penalty. Throttling and limiting are listed as penalties too, and the policy says nothing about whether a user is told when one is applied. [1] That matters more than it sounds, because of how AI is sold. Developers pay by the token. People on monthly plans pay a flat fee for a usage limit they never see in tokens at all. Either way, the number a user watches is not money, and decades of research on payment says that when the number is not money, people spend more and feel it less. Credit cards and casino chips work the same way. [11] Tokens are the unit because that is what computing time costs, so this is not a trick. But it has the same effect as one. A system that already keeps cost one step removed from the user can slow or shrink what that user gets while the fee stays the same, and the user may never see it.This is not hypothetical. On April 23 of this year Anthropic published a postmortem on six weeks of worse output in Claude Code and two related products. The company had lowered the default amount of thinking the model does, to cut waiting time, at a small cost in quality. Two other product changes also hurt the output. The API, where customers pay per token, was not affected. Anthropic reverted the changes, said it never intentionally degrades its models, and reset everyone’s usage limits. [12] It had said the same thing in September 2025, after a separate set of bugs. [13] This author takes the company at its word on the model. The point is what the user could see, which was nothing. For six weeks, people paying a flat monthly fee got less than they had been getting, and nothing on the screen told them whether it was a bug, a cost decision, or a penalty. The new policy makes the third possibility official and still does not say how a user would know.Opinion 2. So the second level comes out mixed, and the fix is cheap. Anthropic would pass the full test by putting the appeal path in the policy itself, saying what a suspended user will be told, giving a timeline, even a long one, and stating plainly when a user is being slowed down. None of that requires a new policy. It requires four sentences that a person can check.Opinion 3. There is a fair objection to all this, and it should be stated plainly. A company that shows too much gives away things it needs to keep. Detection methods, once published, teach the next actor how to avoid them. Details of the model are the product itself. And a company that speaks plainly about its own enforcement invites a round of criticism that a quieter company avoids. All of that is true, and it is why the test matters. The test does not ask a company to open everything. It asks for the specific things the affected person needs to check: the standard, the reason, the date, the notice. None of those is a trade secret. A reason for a suspension does not reveal how the suspicion was formed. A notice that a user is being slowed does not reveal how the model works. The one place the objection really bites is the casebook, and Anthropic’s own reports show the line can be drawn: the cases are public, the detection details mostly are not. That leaves the trade-off where it belongs, as a cost rather than a barrier. In a period of low inferred trust in Big Tech, this author’s view is that the cost is one the frontier labs have to pay. It is the price of staying credible, and it is cheaper than the alternative, which is rules written by people who were given nothing to check.Each level needs the same pair: a right to check and a limit on checking.The governmentAt the third level, lawmakers and agencies infer whether the labs and their users can be trusted, and decide how soon to write rules. Here the picture is harder, because the checking runs both ways and both sides can fail. A lab can conceal. The spring quality problem came to light through user complaints, not through anything the company published first, [14] and California’s new frontier AI law has to rely on whistleblowers to learn whether companies are following it, because the state cannot see inside. [15] A government can overreach. The same power that lets an agency demand an incident report lets it demand the conversations of every user or the model itself, and a lever like that is rarely used only for the purpose it was built for. And governments are users too. The usage policy allows tailored contracts for government customers whose missions the standard rules would block, so the body doing the checking at this level is also one of the parties being checked at the first. [1]So the test at this level has two halves: what can a government check, and how much checking should it be allowed? A workable answer already exists in outline. California’s SB 53 asks the largest developers to publish their safety frameworks, report serious incidents to the state within fifteen days, and protect employees who speak up. It asks for disclosure, not control, and it does not reach into user data or the model’s inner workings. [16] Anthropic endorsed it, saying the law formalizes practices it already follows and lets developers compete while still disclosing the capabilities that pose risks to public safety. [17] That is checking scoped to what a government needs in order to write good rules, and no wider.The high-risk half of Anthropic’s announced policy update shows the relationship from the other side. It lists eleven areas, among them employment, credit, insurance, housing, education, and public benefits, and requires that a qualified person review the AI’s recommendation and that the affected person be told AI was used. [1] That language closely parallels regulation. Colorado’s 2026 replacement framework covers automated decision-making technology used to materially influence consequential decisions in education, employment, housing, financial or lending services, insurance, healthcare, and essential government services and public benefits. Its new requirements include consumer notice, an explanation after an adverse decision, and a right to request meaningful human review and reconsideration. [18] The European Union’s list adds migration, asylum, and the courts. So when Anthropic’s announced update calls for human review and disclosure in employment decisions, it reflects the same concern lawmakers have put into law: the person affected needs a way to check the decision. That is the system working. The pace is the hard part. Colorado first delayed its earlier law to June 30, 2026. It then enacted SB26-189 on May 14, replacing that framework with new requirements beginning January 1, 2027. The implementing rules are still being developed. [19] Lawmakers are revising both the content of the rules and the time allowed to put them into practice.Waiting has a cost of its own, and AT&T is the record of it. Anthropic, like most of Big Tech, stands at the crossroads of a utility and a for-profit company, and AT&T stood there for most of a century. It was private, it was essential, and for decades it answered mostly to itself. It held back inventions that threatened its business; a working answering machine built in its own labs in 1934 was shelved because the company feared that recorded calls would hurt the telephone. [20] The government made it a regulated monopoly in the 1910s and 1930s, which mostly protected it, and when the government finally changed course, the remedy was to break the company apart in 1984. A company at that crossroads is tempted to hold information back and shape the rest, and the longer the rules wait, the more they cost when they arrive.The three togetherPut the three levels together and the same pattern shows at each. Trust is no longer assumed. It is inferred, from evidence. At every level, the evidence sits with the lab: it sees what users do, it knows why it suspended someone, it knows what a regulator would need to see. And at every level, the checker can reach too far: a company watching its users more closely than it needs to, a public demanding the product itself, a government demanding the conversations. So each level needs the same pair, a right to check and a limit on checking.Opinion 4. For the labs, the most useful thing is to show their work before they are made to: publish what they catch, write down the standard they use, give a reason and a date to the people they cut off, and say when they slow someone down. For lawmakers, it is to ask for what they need in order to write rules and confirm they are followed, and nothing wider, and to do it at the right pace: early enough to prevent the harms that can already be seen, not so early or so heavy that it prevents the thing itself. It is a hard balance. It is also the one on which AI’s future depends.References[1] Anthropic, Usage Policy, effective November 12, 2026. https://www.anthropic.com/legal/aup[2] Anthropic, Usage Policy, effective September 15, 2025, archived. https://www.anthropic.com/legal/archive/22742366-2ef0-4c7a-a833-6523f10d3944[3] Unite.AI, “Anthropic Details Disrupted Claude Misuse Across Seven Harm Areas,” September 2026. https://unite.ai/anthropic-details-disrupted-claude-misuse-across-seven-harm-areas[4] Mobile World Live, “Anthropic flags AI weapons misuse,” September 2026. https://mobileworldlive.com/ai-cloud/anthropic-flags-ai-weapons-misuse[5] Northeast Times, report on Anthropic’s September 2026 threat intelligence report. https://northeasttimes.com/?p=163202[6] The Next Web, “Anthropic details how Claude was misused for surveillance and weapons,” September 2026. https://thenextweb.com/news/anthropic-claude-misuse-threat-intelligence-report[7] Anthropic Support, “Trust and safety warnings and appeals.” https://support.claude.com/en/articles/8241253-trust-and-safety-warnings-and-appeals[8] AI Weekly, “Anthropic gates Claude ban appeals behind a login-only form.” https://aiweekly.co/alerts/anthropic-gates-claude-ban-appeals-behind-a-login-only-form[9] User bug reports, June 11 and July 1, 2026. https://claudeissues.com/issue/67332-bug-account-suspended-without-clear-reason-appeal-form-broken-no-working-appeal and https://claudeissues.com/issue/72668-bug-appeal-page-is-inaccessible-for-suspended-pro-accounts-restricted-redirects[10] Kashmir Hill, “A Dad Took Photos of His Naked Toddler for the Doctor. Google Flagged Him as a Criminal,” The New York Times, August 21, 2022. https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/21/technology/google-surveillance-toddler-photo.html[11] Priya Raghubir and Joydeep Srivastava, “Monopoly Money: The Effect of Payment Coupling and Form on Spending Behavior,” Journal of Experimental Psychology: Applied 14, no. 3 (2008): 213-225; Drazen Prelec and Duncan Simester, “Always Leave Home Without It: A Further Investigation of the Credit-Card Effect on Willingness to Pay,” Marketing Letters 12, no. 1 (2001): 5-12.[12] Anthropic, “An update on recent Claude Code quality reports,” April 23, 2026. https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/april-23-postmortem[13] Anthropic status incident, “Model output quality,” September 2025, as quoted at https://simonwillison.net/2025/Sep/9/anthropic-model-output-quality/[14] AI Catchup, “Anthropic’s Claude Code Post-Mortem: Three Engineering Missteps Behind the Spring 2026 Quality Decline.” https://aicatchup.com/news/claude-code-quality-postmortem[15] AI Safety Newsletter 63, “California’s SB-53 Passes the Legislature,” September 2025. AI Safety NewsletterAI Safety Newsletter #63: California’s SB-53 Passes the LegislatureWelcome to the AI Safety Newsletter by the Center for AI Safety. We discuss developments in AI and AI safety. No technical background required…[16] Implicator, “California’s SB 53 puts AI labs on the record.” https://www.implicator.ai/californias-sb-53-puts-ai-labs-on-the-record/[17] Anthropic, “Anthropic is endorsing SB 53,” September 8, 2025, as summarized at https://www.longtermwiki.com/resources/523ff672174f35cf[18] Colorado General Assembly, “SB26-189: Automated Decision-Making Technology,” signed May 14, 2026. https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189[19] Colorado General Assembly, “SB25B-004: Increase Transparency for Algorithmic Systems,” approved August 28, 2025. https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb25b-004; Colorado Attorney General, “Colorado Automated Decision-Making Technology & Chatbot Safety Rulemaking.” https://coag.gov/ai/[20] Tim Wu, The Master Switch: The Rise and Fall of Information Empires (New York: Knopf, 2010).[21] John Elijah, “Data Centers Have Exposed Big Tech’s Trust Deficit,” Substack, October 2026.Facts are sourced. Opinions are marked."}, {"title": "Data Centers Have Exposed Big Tech’s Trust Deficit", "subtitle": "Americans used the products for twenty years without trusting the companies. A data center is the first thing they could vote on.", "topic": "Institutions & trust", "url": "/essays/data-centers-have-exposed-big-techs/", "text": "A hearing notice outside a data center. Illustration by the author.The fight over data centers is only partly about data centers. The buildings stand in for AI, and AI stands in for something older, which is the trust Americans once gave the technology companies without asking for proof. That trust used to be assumed. Now it has to be inferred from what the companies do, and the inference is fraying. This essay argues that the opposition to data centers is best read as the public’s answer to that longer history, given at the one place where these companies have to ask permission.The signalWhat needs explaining is how quickly and how widely opinion has turned. In the summer of 2025 voters were evenly split on having a data center built near them, and a year later three in four were opposed [1]. Seven in ten Americans now oppose one in their own area, including clear majorities of Democrats, independents, and Republicans [2]. In Ohio the Republican and Democratic nominees for governor both want to pause new projects [3]. Americans agree on very little right now, so when people who disagree about nearly everything else agree on this, the agreement is a signal, and the question is what it is a signal of.The two usual answers do not go far enough. One holds that the complaints about water, electricity, and noise are exaggerated [4], and some of them may be. The effect on household bills so far looks small in most places, though prices have jumped on the mid-Atlantic grid, partly because of data center demand [5][6]. The other answer holds that the opposition is manufactured by foreign adversaries. Some of the online activity is, but what has been documented is small and its effect has been overstated [7]. Both questions are worth settling on the facts. Neither one explains how three in four voters came to the same view in a year.The first stand-inA better explanation begins with what kind of thing a data center is. AI is hard to see, even for people who use it every day, and a data center is the one part of it that sits in a particular place, where it can be seen, measured, and voted on. Others have already said that the buildings have become a stand-in for AI itself [8][9][10], and the evidence gives them some support. Only 8 percent of opponents know of a data center anywhere near them [8]. Americans are more opposed to a data center in their area than to a nuclear plant [2]. When opponents explain themselves in their own words, about one in eight talk about AI taking jobs, and a similar share say they dislike or distrust AI [2]. Neither has anything to do with a building down the road.That reading is only part of the truth. Most opponents still name water, power, land, and bills, and one pollster who looked at the reasons people give concluded that the idea “isn’t exactly right” [11]. But it points in the right direction, because people are bringing more to the building than the building itself explains.The second stand-inThis essay goes one step further. If the buildings stand in for AI, then AI stands in for distrust of the companies that build it, and that distrust is much older than AI. Others have pointed toward this as well [9][12], and an earlier essay argued that the change people fear from AI began years ago, with the smartphone and social media [13]. What this essay adds is an account of what happened to the trust.The public debate has stayed in the left box. This essay is about the right one.It helps to separate two kinds. Assumed trust is given in advance, before anyone checks. Inferred trust is worked out from what a person can observe: what a company did last time, whether it fixed what went wrong, and whether anyone outside the company can verify what it says. A forthcoming book, Governance, Bureaucracy, and Organization, draws the same line about competence in organizations [14], and it carries over to trust.For years the technology companies enjoyed the first kind. In 2015 seven in ten Americans thought these companies were good for the country, and by 2019 only half did [15]. Confidence in them has since fallen to the lowest level since Gallup began asking in 2020 [16]. Each party has taken its turn, Republicans first and Democrats now [16][17], for different reasons and in the same direction. So AI did not arrive to a public ready to assume the best. Half a year after ChatGPT appeared, more than eight in ten Americans, in both parties, already said they did not trust the companies developing AI to do it responsibly [18].The puzzle is that almost everyone kept using the products while the trust drained away. Nine in ten adults now own a smartphone [19], and nearly half of teenagers say social media is bad for people their age [20]. One experiment shows how both can be true. College students put a real value on their own access to TikTok, and yet on average they were willing to pay to have it switched off for everyone on campus, themselves included [21]. They used it because everyone else did. Albert Hirschman observed that a person unhappy with an organization can leave or can speak up [22]. With phones and social media most people could do neither, because leaving meant losing touch with family, school, and work, and there was never a meeting to speak at. These products arrived one phone at a time, and nobody held a vote.Smartphone ownership kept rising while the public’s view of the technology companies fell.So people stayed, but they stopped taking the companies’ good faith for granted and began judging them by what they could see. That is the move from assumed trust to inferred trust, and it explains how use could keep rising while trust kept falling. The same pattern is already visible with AI, where about half of adults now use chatbots and most have little confidence that the companies will handle the technology responsibly [23].The inference is frayingInferred trust depends on evidence, and the evidence the public has been given this year is poor. On March 4, seven companies signed a pledge at the White House to pay for the power their data centers need [24]. Two of them were Meta and Google. Three weeks later one jury found that Meta had knowingly harmed children’s mental health, and another found Meta and YouTube negligent toward a young woman who had started on their platforms as a child [25]. Both companies dispute the verdicts. A pledge is a statement about the future that asks to be believed, which means it asks for assumed trust, and this one is mostly not yet backed by rules that can be enforced [26]. Two months later three in four Americans, including majorities of both parties, said they were not confident the companies would keep it [27].That is what a frayed inference looks like. The companies made a promise aimed directly at the stated complaint, and the public’s answer was that it did not believe them. Opposition to data centers kept climbing through the spring [1]. No poll tests this whole chain, and this essay cannot prove that distrust of the companies is what turns people against a building. But the pieces fit. The distrust is older than AI, both parties share it, and it showed itself the moment the companies asked to be taken at their word.The speed of AI makes this worse. Most of what people hear about it is forecast, some of it bright and some of it dark, and the experts disagree. Much of it also comes from the companies themselves. A public that trusted the companies might be willing to wait and see, but this one has little trust left to draw on.These products arrived one phone at a time, and nobody held a vote.Why it lands on a buildingFor almost twenty years these companies did not need the public’s permission for much, because a phone or an app is adopted by one person at a time. A data center is different. It needs land, water, power, and a permit, and the permit is decided at a public meeting close to home. That makes it one of the few things these companies need that a community can turn down. When the question finally came to a vote, people brought those twenty years with them.What followsIf this is right, much of the debate is aimed at the wrong things. Checking the water figures treats the stated complaint as the whole complaint, and so does searching for bots. Both are worth doing, and neither touches the distrust.Opinion. It also means that most of what the companies are doing does not repair the trust. A pledge is a promise, and a fund for the towns that host the buildings [28] is a gift. Both may be sincere, but both ask for assumed trust, and in this essay’s opinion assumed trust is gone for good, because people who have learned to check rarely go back to not checking. Inferred trust can be rebuilt, but only slowly, and only from conduct that outsiders can see and verify.The public seems to know this. Asked what would raise their confidence in the pledge, people most often named laws that would require the companies to keep it, followed by checks from the government or from independent groups [27]. Some of that has begun, from outside the companies. This summer the Democratic governor of Pennsylvania barred state agencies from signing nondisclosure agreements on data center projects [29], and the Republican governor of Texas ordered an audit and paused new grid connections [30].What Americans said would raise their confidence in the companies’ pledge to pay for their own power.A county board cannot settle any of this. It can say yes or no to a building, but it has no say over what these companies do with AI, or over what they did in the years before it. That larger question belongs with legislatures, regulators, and the companies themselves.None of this is a criticism of AI. It does not say that people who oppose data centers are confused, or that people who use these products are hypocrites, and it is not a forecast of who wins these fights. The argument over water, power, and noise should continue, and it should be settled on the facts. But the people in those county meetings were asked about a building, and the size and speed of their answer suggest they were answering a larger question, one that had been growing for almost twenty years.If this essay helped you see the argument differently, subscribe free to On Borrowed Times for new essays on institutions, merit, and AI. New posts arrive directly in your inbox.ReferencesRobinson Meyer, “Exclusive: 75% of Americans Now Oppose Local Data Center Development,” Heatmap News, August 19, 2026.Jeffrey M. Jones, “Americans Oppose AI Data Centers in Their Area,” Gallup, May 13, 2026.Riley Callanan, “Data center boom puts Republicans in a bind before midterm elections,” CBS News, October 12, 2026.“The moral panic over data centres is foolish,” The Economist, September 3, 2026.Kiran Garimella, “Are Data Centers Raising Your Electric Bill? Mostly Not. Yet.,” New Jersey State Policy Lab, Rutgers University, June 22, 2026.Energy and Environmental Economics (E3), “Understanding the Drivers of Rising Electricity Rates and the Role of Data Centers,” May 2026, prepared for the Data Center Coalition.Loreben Tuquero, “Is US data center opposition a ‘China PR thing,’ as Trump says? Experts say that’s exaggerated,” PolitiFact, September 23, 2026.Megan Morrone, “Data centers become the face of AI backlash,” Axios, June 22, 2026, reporting a Milltown Partners poll. Milltown advises AI companies.Tom Wheeler, “Data center backlash signals a fight over AI power,” Brookings, July 7, 2026.“Data center backlash is proxy for discomfort toward AI, says author and professor Walter Isaacson,” CNBC (video), September 4, 2026.UMass Amherst Poll, “Just 1 in 9 Americans Support AI Data Center Construction in their Local Communities,” September 14, 2026.Brian Merchant, “With the backlash to data centers, Flock and AI glasses, a mass opposition to big tech is underway,” Blood in the Machine, August 27, 2026.John Elijah, “Wrong Date, Wrong Institution, Wrong Scale,” Substack, October 2026.Governance, Bureaucracy, and Organization: Stewardship, Drift, and Administrative Capacity (Routledge, forthcoming).“Americans’ views on tech firms turn sharply negative: poll,” AFP via Tech Xplore, July 2019, reporting Pew Research Center figures.Lydia Saad, “Confidence in U.S. Institutions Remains Near All-Time Low,” Gallup, July 13, 2026.Lydia Saad, “Confidence in Big Business, Big Tech Wanes Among Republicans,” Gallup, July 19, 2021.Mallory Newall, Johnny Sawyer, and Bernard Mendez, “Few Americans trust the companies developing AI systems to do so responsibly,” Ipsos, July 27, 2023.Pew Research Center, “Mobile Fact Sheet,” November 20, 2025.Pew Research Center, “Teens, Social Media and Mental Health,” April 22, 2025.Leonardo Bursztyn, Benjamin R. Handel, Rafael Jiménez-Durán, and Christopher Roth, “Product market traps in social media: Evidence and policy implications,” Brookings, June 2, 2026.Albert O. Hirschman, Exit, Voice, and Loyalty: Responses to Decline in Firms, Organizations, and States (Harvard University Press, 1970).Allison Steffens Herrera, “Half of Americans now use AI chatbots, but 40% think AI will make society worse and two-thirds don’t trust the government to regulate it,” The Next Web, June 17, 2026, reporting a Pew Research Center survey.The White House, “Fact Sheet: President Donald J. Trump Advances Energy Affordability with the Ratepayer Protection Pledge,” March 4, 2026.Sarah Shamim, “Jury finds Meta, YouTube liable for social media addiction: What we know,” Al Jazeera, March 26, 2026.David M. Klaus and Mark MacCarthy, “The pledge to protect ratepayers from AI data center costs needs enforcement,” Brookings, July 9, 2026.Consumer Reports, “New Survey finds Americans are Skeptical of Big Tech’s Pledge to Pay for All AI Data Center Energy Costs,” June 23, 2026.Jim VandeHei and Mike Allen, “Behind the Curtain: The new existential threat to AI,” Axios, August 19, 2026.Katie Meyer, “What Shapiro’s new data center order does — and doesn’t — force developers to do,” Spotlight PA, August 25, 2026.Emily Medeiros, “Abbott Orders Audit of Texas Data Centers, Pausing Future Grid Connections,” The Daily Signal, August 6, 2026."}, {"title": "You Are Not the User", "subtitle": "What AI does when someone else is using it on you", "topic": "Technology & society", "url": "/essays/you-are-not-the-user/", "text": "An illustration of this essay’s argument, not a claim about any one store.On September 25 the chief executive of Walmart published a letter to his customers. “We price the product, not the person,” John Furner wrote. He promised that a shopper’s income, shopping history, or urgency would not change a price.Ten days later Walmart published a second letter. It was addressed to Lindsay Owens, who runs a progressive research group in Washington called Groundwork Collaborative and had just published a book on pricing technology. The letter asked her to correct the record. The same day her group released a report on Walmart’s patents.One of those patents was granted in 2023. It covers changing the price on a store’s electronic shelf label according to what a shopper has already put in the cart. The text gives an example: “if a customer has tuna fish, they may be offered a different price for mayonnaise.” Walmart replied that a patent describes what could be built, and that “a patent isn’t a plan.” It said tuna in a cart does not change what it charges for mayonnaise, and that it will not renew the patent.What the record supportsThis essay checked what it could. Walmart has said in writing, twice, that it does not set prices by person and will not. This essay found no one who has shown otherwise, and it accepts that record. The fact-checking site Snopes examined one widely shared claim in September and reported no credible evidence that Walmart uses customer data to set individual prices.Several things are documented. Walmart is replacing paper price tags with digital labels. It runs an AI shopping assistant called Sparky, and its executives have told investors that customers who use it spend about 40 percent more per order. The company says that figure does not mean anyone paid more for the same item. Its privacy notice says it uses license plate readers on its properties for security. It holds the patents, and two have the words “dynamic pricing” in the title.Some claims did not hold up when checked. This essay found no evidence that parking lot cameras are used to set prices, or that a price-setting shopping cart is being tested.Room for doubtAccepting the record does not close the question.Walmart is right that a patent is not a plan. Companies file many patents and use few. But a patent is also a choice. It costs money, and it means someone inside the company thought the idea was worth owning.These ideas were more than a single filing. One of the dynamic pricing patents grew out of an application made in 2014. It was granted in 2021, and it covers adjusting an online price using, among other things, a shopper’s past behavior. In July 2024 Walmart paid the fee to keep it in force. The shelf label application of 2018 described pricing by what is in a shopper’s cart, but it did not ask for protection of that idea. By the time the patent was granted in 2023, the cart was at the center of what it protects. Walmart said it would not renew that patent the day after the Groundwork report appeared. This essay found no such statement about the others.There is also the question of incentive. A pledge asks a company to leave a profitable tool unused, while its executives tell investors that the shopping assistant raises what customers spend. Phil Lempert, a longtime grocery analyst, wrote in October that he believes Furner. He added that the promise will be worth only as much as the next chief executive decides.And there is the question of what can be seen. On a phone or a website, each shopper sees only their own screen. If a seller did price the person there, the buyer could not tell. So the fact that no one has caught it says less than it seems to.Opinion 1. This essay’s opinion is that skepticism about intentions is fair here. Skepticism is not an accusation. It is the ordinary response to a party that has the tools and the incentive, and whose conduct is hard to watch from outside.Not only WalmartThe same doubt reaches other large institutions. The evidence comes in three strengths, and it helps to keep them apart.Some conduct has been shown. In December 2025 Consumer Reports, working with Groundwork and a third group, published the results of five tests at stores in four cities. In each test, a group of volunteers added the same items to their Instacart carts, from the same store, at the same time. More than 400 volunteers took part. About three quarters of the items appeared at more than one price, and the gap on a single item reached 23 percent. Instacart said the tests were random, and it ended them two weeks later. Governments belong on this list. The Dutch tax authority used algorithms to pick families for fraud checks, and it counted a second nationality against them. Thousands of families were wrongly accused, and the government resigned over it in 2021.Some conduct has been charged or announced. In 2024 the Justice Department sued a software company called RealPage. It alleged that landlords gave the company their private rent data and that its algorithm sent back recommended prices. “Training a machine to break the law is still breaking the law,” the deputy attorney general said. RealPage agreed to a settlement in 2025 without admitting liability. In July 2025 Delta told investors it planned to use AI pricing across a fifth of its domestic network by the end of that year. Three senators wrote to Delta with concerns that fares would be set by person. Delta answered that it had never used, tested, or planned such fares.And some is left open. That is where Walmart belongs. The tools exist, the pledge exists, and an outsider cannot readily check which one the company follows.The amplifierNone of this conduct began with AI. For most of history a price was whatever a buyer and a seller could agree on. The Quakers were among the first merchants to commit to one fixed price for everyone, and they did it on moral grounds. Sellers went on trying to learn what customers would buy and what they would pay. In 2004 The New York Times reported that Walmart’s data showed sales of strawberry Pop-Tarts rising to seven times the normal rate ahead of a hurricane. In 2012 The Wall Street Journal found the Staples website showing different prices for the same stapler, depending on where it estimated the shopper was.What AI changes is the shape. A clerk could judge one customer at a time. A pricing system can do it for every shopper and every item. In January 2025 the staff of the Federal Trade Commission reported that firms selling pricing tools had at least 250 clients. It said details as small as a shopper’s mouse movements could be used to tailor a price.This first effect has a name. In 2015 the computer scientist Kentaro Toyama called it the law of amplification: technology’s main effect is to amplify human forces that are already there. That includes the good ones. The kind of engine that could raise a price can also lower one.The mufflerThe second effect gets less attention. The same systems can make it harder to see who is acting.One kind hides who is influencing a purchase. Instacart’s own website said shoppers “are not aware that they’re in an experiment.” It took groups of volunteers shopping at the same moment to find it, because one shopper sees one price. In the old haggling, both sides knew a negotiation was going on. Here only one side knows.The other kind hides who did the work. In January 2025 the Securities and Exchange Commission found that a company called Presto had claimed its AI took drive-through orders without human order-takers. In fact people stepped in on the vast majority of orders. The reverse also happens. In October 2025 Deloitte agreed to refund part of its fee to the Australian government for a report that cited research papers that do not exist. The corrected version disclosed that an AI tool had been used.Others have named parts of this. Dan Davies calls a system that absorbs blame an “accountability sink.” Frank Pasquale wrote of a “black box society” in 2015. Neither effect is this essay’s discovery. Its contribution is to put the two side by side and to ask what each one calls for.The word muffler is chosen with some care. A muffler does not weaken an engine. The engine runs as hard as before, and the people nearby hear less of it. The muffler is also why proof comes late. A practice that is hard to see is hard to prove.What the three answers leave out“Wrong Date, Wrong Institution, Wrong Scale” answered three people who spoke about AI on Bloomberg: Michael Sandel, Daniel Diermeier, and Meghan Sullivan. This essay keeps its respect for all three and adds one observation. Much of the segment concerned people who use AI, or who decide whether to.A shopper at the shelf is not the user. The store is. The same is true of a renter, a passenger, and a family applying for a benefit.Opinion 2. That changes how far each answer reaches. Sandel fears a change in what it means to be human, and the earlier essay agreed that something human is being lost. This essay’s opinion is that the harm described here is nearer. It has dates and named parties, and it can reach people who never open a chatbot. Diermeier wants students who can think for themselves. A shopper can think very well and still not see how a price was set. Sullivan came closest. She said ordinary people are not helpless. But how a store sets its prices is not the shopper’s choice to make. It belongs to the store, and to whoever writes the rules for stores.Where the rules are being writtenSome of those rules now exist. Maryland’s ban on using a shopper’s personal data to charge more for groceries took effect on October 1. Connecticut and New Jersey have enacted bans of their own. New York’s legislature passed one in June, and the governor had not signed it when this was written. In Washington, a Republican, Josh Hawley, chaired a Senate hearing on the practice in August. A Democrat on the panel, Richard Blumenthal, described “a bipartisan feeling of urgency.” A bill in the House is led by a Democrat and a Republican.This essay should not oversell any of it. No federal bill has passed. The three state laws come from states led by Democrats, and Colorado’s Democratic governor vetoed a similar bill. Consumer Reports said Maryland’s law has too many loopholes to protect shoppers. Grocers’ trade groups say their members do not price by person, and they warn that broad rules could reduce loyalty discounts.What matters here is the target. These laws do not regulate a model, a university, or a member of the public. They regulate what an institution may do to a person with a machine, and they give someone outside the institution the power to check. That is the scale the earlier essay asked for.Builders and usersTwo arguments about AI rules are often treated as one. They should be kept apart.The first is about the labs that build the largest models, and how tightly to regulate them. That question is not easy. Anyone who says it is easy is not counting what it would cost, at home and abroad, to slow AI too much. The earlier essay noted two facts that bear on this. Reports cited in the Bloomberg segment put AI investment at about 2 percent of the American economy, and China has set national targets for AI adoption. Too little restraint has costs of its own. This author welcomes that argument and thinks it has to happen. He does not yet have an opinion on where the line belongs. Wherever it is drawn, it will have costs and benefits. No line is free.Opinion 3. Whatever is decided there, this essay’s main point is about where the burden of conduct should sit. It should not sit mostly on individuals. That asks people to regulate, one at a time, institutional conduct they cannot see. It should not sit mostly on the labs either. The labs are coming to look like utilities. Sam Altman, who runs OpenAI, has said he sees a future where intelligence is “a utility, like electricity or water.” A power company answers for the safety of its own lines. It does not answer for what every customer does with the current. To put too much of the burden on the labs is to ask them to hold back development, in ways that could work against national security and against legitimate uses of the tools.Opinion 4. That leaves the second argument, which is about the governments and companies that use AI on other people. It does not matter whether they built the model or bought it from a lab. Here this essay does have an opinion. The burden belongs with them, and the time for legislation is now, before the harm is proved in every case.Opinion 5. The reason is simple. “The Durable Organization,” another essay by this author, argues that change put off does not go away. It arrives later as one large change, under pressure. This essay’s opinion is that rules work the same way. The longer institutions are allowed to amplify their reach and muffle their conduct in unethical or manipulative ways, the harder that will be to unwind. Instacart ended its price tests two weeks after they became public. By its own account, those tests involved a small number of retailers. A practice that has spread across an industry, with revenue depending on it, will be much harder to end.These rules are not free either. Drafted badly, they could reduce ordinary discounts, as the grocers warn. But they bar particular conduct, and a company that already keeps its pledge gives up little when the pledge becomes law.Two questionsThe amplifier and the muffler give anyone two questions to ask about a use of AI. What is it making stronger? What is it making harder to see?What is it making stronger? What is it making harder to see?The answers call for different remedies. Where a practice has grown too strong, the remedy is a limit, such as a ban on setting a price from personal data. Where something has gone out of sight, the remedy is to bring it back into view. That can mean a label on the price and a record an outsider can inspect. It also means a named person who answers for the result.“The Truth Will Set You Free,” another earlier essay, offered a test for any use of these machines. Does it leave people able to understand, to check, and to answer for a decision? A muffled system fails the second part, and often the third. That essay also argued that announcements rarely change what people are rewarded for doing. A pledge is an announcement. Walmart’s letter is an institution binding itself in public, and that deserves credit. A promise that outsiders cannot readily check is not yet a rule.What this essay is againstThis essay is not against capitalism. It is not against profit, low prices, or the tools. The same engines can mark down a price or catch a fraud.Opinion 6. It is against one thing. That is any institution, a company or a government, using AI to amplify unethical conduct while muffling it for the people it is used on. One fair test of conduct is whether it could be announced to the people it affects. Instacart’s price tests ended two weeks after they became public.That conduct is old. AI changes its shape. It reaches more people, and it is harder to see. Proof exists in some of the cases above and not in others. Where there is none, this essay claims only that the doubt is legitimate, and that the answer to legitimate doubt is a way to check.A scapegoatOpinion 7. In an interview published in early October, Altman told Vanity Fair that people anxious about AI “need a sink for the anxiety, a scapegoat.” He is probably right that anyone in his job would be treated the same way. This essay’s guess is that the muffler is part of the reason. When people cannot see who is acting on them, the blame tends to go to a person they can name, or to the machine.An institution can point at the machine too. In 2024 Air Canada argued before a Canadian tribunal that it was not liable for what the chatbot on its own website told a customer. The tribunal called that a “remarkable submission” and held the airline responsible.So the better aim is neither Altman nor “AI.” It is the company or the agency that uses a system on people, together with the rule that says what it may do and who answers for it. Whoever uses these tools on other people should have to do it in the open.ReferencesOn Walmart’s two letters and the Groundwork report: John Furner, “A Letter from Our CEO,” Walmart, 25 September 2026. Dan Bartlett, “Correcting the Record: We Price Products, Not People,” Walmart, 5 October 2026. Lindsay Owens and Elizabeth Pancotti, “The Walmart Watchtower: Patents Speak Louder Than Pledges,” Groundwork Collaborative, 5 October 2026. Dani James, “Walmart defends pricing practices, again,” Retail Dive, 6 October 2026.On what is documented about Walmart: Snopes, “Did Walmart update its privacy policy to allow surveillance pricing?” 10 September 2026. Walmart, “Digital Shelf Labels: A Modern Tool for Every Day Low Pricing,” 4 September 2026. Walmart, corrected transcript of its session at the Goldman Sachs Global Retailing Conference, remarks by Dave Guggina, 15 September 2026. Walmart, “Walmart Automated License Plate Readers (ALPR) Privacy Notice,” updated 16 February 2026.On Walmart’s patents: Walmart Apollo, LLC, U.S. Patent 11,687,872 B2, “System and method for remote controlling of electronic shelf labels,” filed 19 March 2018, granted 27 June 2023. Walmart Apollo, LLC, U.S. Patent Application Publication 2018/0276603 A1, “System and method for remote controlling of electronic shelf labels,” published 27 September 2018. Walmart Apollo, LLC, U.S. Patent 10,896,433 B2, “Dynamic pricing systems and methods,” granted 19 January 2021. The patent family’s earliest priority date is 30 April 2014. The legal events list a fourth-year maintenance fee paid on 17 July 2024. Walmart Apollo, LLC, U.S. Patent 11,429,992 B2, “Systems and methods for dynamic pricing,” filed 27 November 2018, granted 30 August 2022. Walmart Apollo, LLC (originally Wal-Mart Stores, Inc.), U.S. Patent Application Publication 2018/0060884 A1, “Vehicle Suspension Measurement System and Associated Methods,” published 1 March 2018. The legal events list a final rejection on 11 May 2020 and abandonment on 5 December 2020.On the claims that did not hold up, and on the pledge: Chris Dickerson, “Retail expert: Claims in ‘fair price’ book unfounded, impractical,” Legal Newsline, 2 October 2026. Phil Lempert, “Who’s Right About Walmart’s Pricing?” The Robin Report, 7 October 2026.On the Instacart tests: Katie J. Wells, Lindsay Owens, Angel Han, and Alan Smith, “Same Cart, Different Price: Instacart’s Price Experiments Cost Families at Checkout,” Groundwork Collaborative and Consumer Reports, 9 December 2025. Consumer Reports, “New Report Exposes Instacart’s Hidden Price Games,” press release, 9 December 2025. NBC News, “Instacart to halt ‘price tests’ amid scrutiny of its AI tool for retailers,” December 2025.On the Dutch childcare benefits case: DutchNews.nl, “Tax office fined €2.7m for discriminating against dual national families,” 8 December 2021. European Parliament, “The Dutch childcare benefit scandal, institutional racism and algorithms,” question for oral answer O-000028/2022, 28 June 2022.On RealPage and Delta: U.S. Department of Justice, “Justice Department Sues RealPage for Algorithmic Pricing Scheme that Harms Millions of American Renters,” press release, 23 August 2024. Jeffrey C. Bank and others, “DOJ Settles Its Algorithmic Price-Fixing Case Against RealPage,” Wilson Sonsini Goodrich & Rosati, 1 December 2025. David Shepardson, “Delta plans to use AI in ticket pricing draws fire from US lawmakers,” Reuters, 22 July 2025, as carried by Yahoo Finance. Marlon Hyde, “Delta Air Lines plans to use more AI to price tickets, but what data will it use?” WABE, 31 July 2025.On prices before AI, and on today’s pricing tools: Bronson Arcuri and Benjamin Naddaff-Hafrey, “The Price Tag Hasn’t Always Existed, It Had To Be Invented,” NPR Planet Money, 28 February 2018, as carried by WUWM. Constance L. Hays, “What Wal-Mart Knows About Customers’ Habits,” The New York Times, 14 November 2004. The Wall Street Journal, “Websites Vary Prices, Deals Based on Users’ Information,” December 2012. Federal Trade Commission, “FTC Surveillance Pricing Study Indicates Wide Range of Personal Data Used to Set Individualized Consumer Prices,” press release, 17 January 2025.On amplification, blame, and the black box: Kentaro Toyama, Geek Heresy: Rescuing Social Change from the Cult of Technology, PublicAffairs, 2015. Dan Davies, The Unaccountability Machine: Why Big Systems Make Terrible Decisions and How the World Lost Its Mind, Profile Books, 2024. Frank Pasquale, The Black Box Society: The Secret Algorithms That Control Money and Information, Harvard University Press, 2015.On who did the work: U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, “SEC Charges Restaurant-Technology Company Presto Automation for Misleading Statements About AI Product,” 14 January 2025. Associated Press, “Deloitte to partially refund Australian government for report with apparent AI-generated errors,” 7 October 2025, as carried by the Jamaica Gleaner.On the Bloomberg segment and earlier essays by this author: Bloomberg, report by Chrystia Freeland with Michael Sandel, Daniel Diermeier, and Meghan Sullivan, as posted by RealClearPolitics under the headline “Philosopher Michael Sandel: The Real AI Threat Is Changing What It Means To Be Human,” 5 October 2026. John Elijah, “Wrong Date, Wrong Institution, Wrong Scale,” October 2026. John Elijah, “The Truth Will Set You Free,” October 2026. John Elijah, “The Durable Organization,” October 2026.On the state laws: Consumer Reports, “Consumer Reports statement on the signing of Maryland’s Protection from Predatory Pricing Act into law,” 28 April 2026. The Wise Marketer, “Surveillance Pricing, State by State: The Complete Legislative Tracker,” 11 September 2026. Electronic Privacy Information Center, “Connecticut Is Second State to Enact Surveillance Pricing Ban,” 4 June 2026. The Shelby Report, “NJ Gov. Signs Surveillance Pricing Ban Despite Industry Objections,” 24 July 2026. Spectrum News NY1, “NYC businesses urge Hochul to back changes to bill banning ‘surveillance pricing,’” 12 August 2026. New York State Senate, Senate Bill S8623B, 2025-2026 session, “Prohibits the use of surveillance pricing,” bill record as read on 8 October 2026. ICSC, “Surveillance Pricing Legislation Gains Momentum as States Wrap Up 2026 Sessions,” 9 July 2026.On Congress and the grocers’ trade groups: Consumer Finance Monitor, “Senate Judiciary Hearing Reveals Bipartisan Support for Federal Action on AI-Driven ‘Surveillance Pricing,’” 14 August 2026. Rev, “Senate Hearing on Surveillance Pricing,” transcript of the 4 August 2026 hearing of the Senate Judiciary Subcommittee on Crime and Counterterrorism. H.R. 8895, No Rigged Grocery Prices Act, 119th Congress, introduced 19 May 2026 by Rep. Josh Gottheimer (D-N.J.) with Rep. Michael Lawler (R-N.Y.). Lorrie Griffith, “FMI, NGA Push Back After Senate Hearing On AI Surveillance Pricing,” The Shelby Report, 6 August 2026. Joshua Mitchell, “NJ Grocery Shoppers Can Sue Retailers for Surveillance Pricing Under New Law,” Tech Times, 25 July 2026.On China’s targets and on intelligence as a utility: State Council of China, “China issues guideline to accelerate ‘AI Plus’ integration across key sectors,” 27 August 2025. AJ Dellinger, “Sam Altman Says Intelligence Will Be a Utility, and He’s Just the Man to Collect the Bills,” Gizmodo, 12 March 2026.On the scapegoat remark and the Air Canada ruling: RealClearPolitics, “Sam Altman on AI Fear: ‘People Need a Scapegoat,’ ‘I Take It Somewhat Personally,’” video and transcript of an interview with Mark Guiducci of Vanity Fair, 7 October 2026. Moffatt v. Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149, British Columbia Civil Resolution Tribunal, 14 February 2024. Proctor (Queensland Law Society), “It wasn’t us, the chatbot did it,” February 2024."}, {"title": "The Durable Organization", "subtitle": "Why strong is not the same as lasting", "topic": "Institutions & trust", "url": "/essays/the-durable-organization/", "text": "Strong is not durable. This illustrates the paper’s argument, not a data chart.The bottom lineStrength and durability are different things. A system can look strong because it refuses to change, and that refusal is what makes it brittle.Political history shows the pattern. Authoritarian states are not weak, and some last a very long time. But they often turn change into a break. In autocracies since the Second World War, about 45 percent of leadership changes brought regime change with them. By Stephen Kotkin’s account, the Soviet Union was stable into the mid-1980s, and the attempt to reform it is what ended it. Established democracies look noisier, and they are among the most stable systems as well. They have channels that turn pressure into small corrections.Organizations show the same pattern, and here it can be measured. Change carries a risk of its own. In one long study, each change raised the odds of failure for a time. Yet firms that change at a steady rhythm have outperformed firms that change in bursts, and declining firms that act early have recovered more often. Change that is put off does not go away. It arrives later as one large change, under pressure.Change is often put off for two reasons. The signals that call for it are not on the instruments leaders watch. And many leaders believe that people resist change, a belief the evidence supports far less than its reputation suggests.This paper does not argue that companies should be run like democracies. It argues that a durable organization keeps those signals in view and changes at the speed of need.Part One: Strong is not durableMost people judge the strength of a system by how much it controls. A state looks strong when its leader has no rivals, its streets are quiet, and its orders are carried out. A company looks strong when it leads its market, its revenue is high, and no competitor is close. In both cases, strong is taken to mean safe.The record does not support that. It does not support the opposite either.Start with states. It is tempting to say that dictatorships are fragile and democracies are stable. The data says something else. A forecasting study led by Jack Goldstone, published in 2010, examined political instability in countries around the world from 1955 to 2003. The risk of instability was lowest at the two ends, in full autocracies and full democracies. The least stable were partial democracies split into factions. Their odds of instability were more than thirty times those of a full autocracy.So authoritarian states are not weak, and some last a very long time. Soviet rule lasted seventy-four years.What differs is the form that change takes. In a democracy, a change of leader is routine. The government changes and the system stays. Autocracies are different. Barbara Geddes, Joseph Wright, and Erica Frantz assembled a record of the 280 autocratic regimes that existed between 1946 and 2010. About 45 percent of the leadership changes in those regimes led to a change of regime. The rest did not, and the same record shows that some kinds of autocracy hand power on more smoothly than others. Still, in such systems, replacing the person at the top often puts the whole structure at risk.Stephen Kotkin, the historian of Russia, made the point in a 2022 interview with The New Yorker. Authoritarian rule, he said, is “all-powerful and brittle at the same time.”That line holds the idea of this paper. The features that make such a system strong are the same features that can make it brittle. A ruler who removes every rival can leave no orderly way to replace him. A system that punishes bad news tells its leaders less and less of the truth. Kotkin makes this point as well. A system that holds everything in place leaves change nowhere to go until it comes all at once.Engineers use two words for what most people treat as one. Strength is the load a material can carry before it gives way. Toughness is how much it can absorb before it breaks. Glass resists great pressure and has very little toughness. It gives no warning before it shatters. Steel bends first. The bend is a warning, and the steel is still in one piece.This paper uses the word durable for that second quality. A durable system can take change and keep going.Opinion 1. Established democracies are durable in this sense, and the reason is not that they are calm. They are noisy. This paper’s reading is that the noise comes from working channels. Elections, courts, a free press, and open argument carry pressure to the people in charge while the pressure is still small. They force small corrections. The name democracy does not do this work. The channels do. Where the channels clog, a democracy stores up change like any other system. An earlier commentary by this paper’s author looked at one form of that clogging, in the administrative machinery of democratic governments. Its title is We Gave Bureaucracy the Fastest Tools in History. It Got Slower.Organizations are judged the way states are. Market share, revenue, headcount, and market value measure strength at one moment. They do not show whether the organization can change. Three companies show the gap.Digital Equipment Corporation was the second largest computer maker, behind only IBM. Its sales reached 14 billion dollars. In 1998 Compaq bought what was left of it.Nokia led the world market for mobile phones for more than a decade. In the second quarter of 2008 it put its own share of that market at 40 percent. In April 2014 it completed the sale of its phone business to Microsoft.General Motors held 54 percent of the American car market in 1954. In June 2009 it filed for bankruptcy.None of these companies was weak at its peak. Two led their fields, and the third was second only to IBM.Digital shows the pattern most plainly. Edgar Schein of MIT consulted to the company from 1966 to 1992 and later wrote its history with three co-authors. Digital’s culture prized creativity and invention, and that culture built the company. By Schein’s account, the same culture later became so fixed that Digital could not adapt, even though it saw the need. What made it strong made it brittle.Nokia and General Motors return later in this paper. Each shows a different part of the argument.This paper does not claim that authoritarian states are weak, or that any of them is close to its end. It does not claim that a democracy is safe because of its name. The claim in this part is narrower. Strength and durability are separate qualities, and the usual measures capture only the first.The next part takes up what happens when change comes late.Part Two: Late change is the dangerous changeIf strong systems are brittle, the obvious advice is to change. That advice is too simple, because change is dangerous too. This part holds both facts together.Start again with states. In Armageddon Averted, published in 2001, Stephen Kotkin argues that the Soviet Union did not have to end when it did. As late as 1985, in his words, it was “lethargically stable.” It had lost the competition with the West, and it could still have carried on for a long time. What ended it, by his account, was the attempt to reform a system that could not be reformed. Neither the leaders nor the public expected reform to end in collapse.Alexis de Tocqueville saw the same pattern in France more than a century before. Writing in 1856 about the fall of the old monarchy, he concluded that a bad government is usually in the greatest danger at the moment it starts to reform. His explanation was about people. They put up with a burden for as long as it seems unavoidable. Once relief looks possible, the same burden becomes unbearable.Opinion 2. Neither writer argues against reform. This paper reads the two cases for what they share. Neither system had stood entirely still. But each had gone a very long time without the change it most needed. When that change was finally attempted, it was very large, and the system had no practice at change of that size.A caution belongs here. A state is not a company, and Tocqueville’s explanation is about how a population reacts. The link this paper draws between states and organizations is an analogy. For organizations there is direct evidence, and it points the same way.Terry Amburgey, Dawn Kelly, and William Barnett studied 1,011 Finnish newspapers over 193 years. They found that a change in a newspaper had two effects. It raised the risk that the paper would fail, and it did so right away. It also made another change of the same kind more likely. Both effects faded with time.Put those findings together. Every change carries a risk. The risk passes if the organization survives it. And an organization that has just changed is more likely to change again.Opinion 3. From here this paper proposes a mechanism. A small change carries a small risk, and each one keeps the organization in practice. A change that is put off does not go away. The need grows. When the change finally comes it is large, and a large change carries a large risk. It also comes at the worst time. The organization is out of practice. Money is short. And the team at the top is often weaker than it was. The studies cited here do not test this mechanism directly. In particular, none shows that practice at small changes makes later change safer.Earlier papers in this series describe two parts of this mechanism. Nothing Stays Tuned explains why the need grows even when nobody decides anything. An organization’s commitments drift, and the gap widens unless someone is watching it. Fast to Cut, Slow to Build describes how large companies fall out of practice. They stay quick at cutting, and they slowly lose the ability to move money and people toward what they need next.The point about the team at the top has evidence behind it. Donald Hambrick and Richard D’Aveni compared 57 large companies that went bankrupt with 57 similar companies that survived. The top teams of the failing companies differed from those of the survivors, and the gap grew wider and faster over the last five years before bankruptcy. The authors propose a spiral that runs both ways. A flawed team makes the company’s trouble worse. The trouble then weakens the team. Executives leave, others are blamed and removed, and the company can no longer attract strong replacements.General Motors shows what late change looks like. Its bankruptcy came at the end of a decline that had lasted three decades. The decline was not hidden, and GM had not stood still. It kept bringing out new products and cutting costs. None of it stopped the slide. GM’s share of the American market fell from 27.3 percent in 2004 to 19.5 percent in mid-2009. By April 2009 it had lost 82 billion dollars since 2004.Then the largest change came all at once. GM filed for bankruptcy on June 1, 2009. A new company began operating on July 10, less than six weeks later. It kept four core brands in the United States and moved to shed four others: Pontiac, Saturn, Hummer, and Saab. More than 40 billion dollars of obligations were removed. The cuts did not end in court. GM said its American workforce would fall from about 91,000 at the end of 2008 to about 64,000 by the end of 2009, with plant and dealer closures to follow. The rescue took about 60 billion dollars of public money, and the governments of the United States, Canada, and Ontario owned 72.5 percent of the new company.A restructuring forced by decades of decline took less than six weeks in court. GM did not control it. GM survived because governments paid. Most organizations have no one to do that.The general idea here is not new, and credit is due. Nassim Taleb and Mark Blyth argued in Foreign Affairs in 2011 that a system whose normal ups and downs are artificially held down becomes very fragile while showing no visible risk. Taleb’s image is a forest. Put out every small fire, and the first large fire that cannot be controlled destroys the whole forest. Gary Hamel and Liisa Välikangas made the point about companies in Harvard Business Review in 2003. A turnaround, in Hamel’s words, is “transformation tragically delayed.”What this paper adds is the join between the two fields, the evidence on timing in the next part, and an explanation of why change gets put off in the first place.So the choice is not between change and safety. It is between small risks taken on time and one large risk taken late. Hold, Collide, Come Apart, another paper in this series, made the same point about the divisions inside a company. The same correction is cheap early and expensive late.The next part looks at what steady change buys, and where that evidence has limits.Part Three: What steady change buysPart Two argued that small risks taken on time are better than one large risk taken late. That is an argument. This part asks what the evidence says. Some good evidence supports the argument, and some evidence complicates it. Both belong here.The strongest support comes from a study of rhythm. Patricia Klarner and Sebastian Raisch followed 67 European insurance companies from 1995 to 2004. They tracked each company’s major changes in strategy and looked at the pattern over time. Some companies changed at regular intervals, with periods of change and periods of stability taking turns. Others changed irregularly. The companies that changed regularly outperformed the ones that changed irregularly. The result held under different conditions inside and outside the companies, and across time periods.Two details of that study matter. First, the finding is about regularity. It is not about changing as often as possible. The authors treat how regularly a company changes and how often it changes as separate things with separate effects. Second, the successful pattern included stability. Their conclusion is that a regular balance between change and stability goes with long-term success.The second piece of support concerns companies already in decline. Chanchai Tangpong, Michael Abebe, and Zonghui Li studied 96 American firms in matched pairs. Declining firms that began to cut back early were more likely to turn around.Now the complications.Opinion 4. The first is inside that same study. What a firm did early mattered. Selling off businesses early helped. Leaving geographic markets early helped. Laying people off early did not. So “act early” is too simple. This paper’s reading is that the early moves that helped were changes to the business itself.The second comes from a larger study. José Luis Barbero and two colleagues examined 263 declining American firms over 26 years, from 1983 to 2009. Early cutbacks helped when the firm’s market was generous, with resources to spare. Early cutbacks hurt when the market was changing fast. In those fast-changing markets, what helped was speed once the cutting began. And in this study an irregular rhythm of cutbacks did better than a regular one. The authors’ overall conclusion still favored acting with urgency. But the details do not line up neatly with the insurance study. The two studies also measured different things. One looked at changes in strategy across a whole industry. The other looked only at cutbacks in firms already in decline.The third is on the side of states. Nassim Taleb and Gregory Treverton argued in Foreign Affairs in 2015 that the best sign of a country’s future is recent moderate volatility, and that a long calm is a poor one. That is this paper’s idea in its boldest form. A reply by Paul Stares in the same journal said the authors had not supported the claim, and that their method did no better than the warning methods already in use. This paper does not rest on that claim.The fourth is a limit on all of these studies. Each covers one kind of firm in one period. Each shows that two things went together. None shows that one caused the other. Companies that change at a regular pace may simply be better run.One finding from Part Two also stands over everything here. In the newspaper study, each change raised the risk of failure. Change is never free. An organization that changes for the sake of changing takes on risk and gets nothing for it.So what does the evidence support? Three modest statements. First, a regular pace of change, with stability in between, went with better long-term performance in the study of rhythm. Second, for firms in decline, acting early on the business itself went with recovery, at least where the market was not changing fast. Third, nothing here supports constant change, and nothing here supports early layoffs.The evidence does not say to change more, or to change faster. It says not to let the need for change run far ahead of the change itself. This paper calls that changing at the speed of need. The phrase comes from an earlier commentary by this paper’s author, which set the speed of need against the speed of red tape. The need sets the pace. Slower than the need, and change piles up. Faster than the need, and the organization pays the risk of change for no reason.That raises the question the rest of this paper is about. If changing on time is better, why do capable leaders so often change late? The usual answer is that they refuse to. This paper gives two other answers. The next part argues that more often they do not see the need in time. The part after it takes up a belief about people that makes delay look wise.Part Four: Why change gets put offThere are two common explanations for late change. One is that leaders see the need and refuse to act. The other is that they do not see the need in time.Research on decline has room for both. A model published in 1989 by William Weitzel and Ellen Jonsson describes decline in five stages. In the first, the organization fails to notice the early signs of trouble. In the second, its managers recognize the need for change and still do nothing. Refusal is the second stage. Not seeing comes first.Opinion 5. This paper’s view is that not seeing is the more common of the two. This paper has not found a study that counts how often each occurs, so this is a judgment and is offered as one.The reason lies in what leaders watch. Revenue, market share, costs, and output are the standard instruments. All of them report results, and results arrive last. Nokia put its share of the world phone market at 40 percent in the second quarter of 2008. The iPhone had gone on sale the year before.An earlier paper in this series, The Unmeasured Layer, argued that a large organization sees its own working condition only in fragments. Different functions each hold a piece. The pieces are rarely assembled into one standing view. How decisions really get made and how work really moves are known inside the organization. They are seldom brought together on the page that leaders read.Trenches, Not Silos, another paper in the series, made a related point. Companies watch customer counts, revenue, and product offerings because those numbers are easy to count. The problems that are hard to count are left to grow.Nokia shows how this plays out, and here the evidence is unusually good. Timo Vuori and Quy Huy conducted 76 interviews with Nokia’s top managers, middle managers, engineers, and outside experts about the years 2005 to 2010. Top managers saw the outside threat clearly. They were afraid of competitors and of shareholders. Middle managers were afraid of something closer, which was their own superiors and peers. Out of that fear they stayed quiet or sent up optimistic reports. Top managers came to believe that Nokia’s technology was stronger than it was, and they pushed harder on that basis. The authors do not say fear was the only cause. They say it was an important one.Nokia’s leaders did not ignore the threat. They saw the competitor. What they could not see was their own company.They saw the competitor. What they could not see was their own company.Part One described the same pattern in a state. A ruler who punishes bad news hears less and less of the truth. The mechanism does not need a dictator. It needs only that telling the truth to the people above carries a cost.That cost is common. Frances Milliken, Elizabeth Morrison, and Patricia Hewlin interviewed 40 employees in a range of industries. Of those, 85 percent said they had at some point held back a concern they thought was important and had not raised it with a supervisor. The study is small and exploratory. Its point is still plain. The knowledge that something is wrong often exists inside an organization and does not travel upward.Opinion 6. There is a second way an organization can learn it is in trouble, and it is quieter. Albert Hirschman described two ways that management finds out about its own failings. People speak up, or people leave. He also gave a warning. The people who care most about quality are the ones best placed to speak up, and they are also the most likely to leave. This paper adds one observation. In a company, the people who can leave most easily are the ones most in demand. When they go, the company loses their work and their warning at once. Their departure is a signal. It is usually recorded as turnover and handed to recruiting.Exit raises a larger argument that this paper does not take up. The philosopher Elizabeth Anderson has compared the modern workplace to a private government, and she holds that the freedom to quit does not change that. Exit may count for more than she allows. People do change jobs, and companies differ a great deal in how they are run. But she is right that exit is uneven. It is most open to those with the most options. For this paper the point is narrower. Each such exit is information, and most organizations do not read it.So the signs that change is needed do exist. They are in what employees know and do not say, in who leaves, and in how the work really moves. These are the vital signs of an organization’s durability. Its results are the signs of its strength. Leaders mostly watch the second kind.This is how not seeing produces late change. By the time the results show the problem, the need is already old, and the change required is already large. Nothing Stays Tuned put the principle in a few words: seeing comes before doing.There is a second reason change gets put off. It is a belief about people, and the next part takes it up.Part Five: What people actually resistThere is a second reason change gets put off. Many leaders believe that people resist change, and that any change will be a fight. A leader who believes this has a reason to make changes rarely, and only when forced. The belief deserves a close look, because the evidence for it is weaker than its reputation.Start with where the idea came from. Eric Dent and Susan Galloway Goldberg traced its history in 1999. The term goes back to Kurt Lewin, and he did not mean it as a fact about employees. He described resistance as a force in the whole system, one that acts on managers and employees alike. Later writers kept the phrase and dropped the meaning. Resistance became a flaw in employees that managers had to overcome. Dent and Goldberg’s conclusion is that people do not resist change itself. They resist losing status, pay, or comfort.The first major study of the subject points the same way. In 1948 Lester Coch and John French studied a pajama factory where production workers were resisting changes to their methods and jobs. The researchers introduced a change to four groups in different ways. One group had no part in planning it. The others took part. The sharpest difference in results was between the group with no part and the groups that took full part. A later review of that work stresses its real claim, which is that resistance comes from the setting in which a change is made and not from the individual.More recent work points the same way, with a caveat. In four studies, Alexandra Michel, Rune Todnem By, and Bernard Burnes examined how a personal tendency to resist change, and the features of a change itself, related to commitment to that change. People were more committed to changes they saw as beneficial. With one exception, the personal tendency did not alter that link. The authors conclude that the level of resistance to any one change depends on the setting and on how the change is managed. They also note that a person’s disposition shapes how change is viewed.Then there is the famous number. It is often said that 70 percent of change efforts fail. Mark Hughes reviewed five published sources for that figure in 2011 and found no valid and reliable evidence behind it. The figure appears to have begun as an estimate in a 1993 book about one kind of change, and the authors of that book called their own estimate unscientific.None of this means that people welcome every change. Experiments by William Samuelson and Richard Zeckhauser showed that people stay with what they already have more often than the choices in front of them justify. A later replication confirmed the effect in three of four tests. The lean toward the familiar is real. It is a lean, though. The same body of research shows people accepting change when they see a benefit in it and have a part in it.There is also direct evidence that people differ. In 2003 Shaul Oreg built and tested a scale that measures a personal inclination to resist change. Scores on it predicted how people reacted to particular changes. One of its four parts is the reaction to change that is imposed.So the common statement is too broad. Some people are more averse to change than others, and that tendency can be measured. But resistance is not a fixed fact about employees. What people resist most reliably is loss, and change that is done to them.What, then, is hard on people? Alannah Rafferty and Mark Griffin identified three features of change that shape how employees react: how often it comes, how large it is, and how well it was planned. In research along these lines, change that was planned went with less uncertainty. Change that came very often went with more uncertainty and with more people intending to leave.A survey record tells a similar story. Gartner reports that in 2016 the average employee went through two planned company-wide changes, and 74 percent of employees were willing to support such change. In 2022 the average employee went through ten, and the willing share had fallen to 43 percent. Two things stand out. About three in four people were willing when the load was light. And willingness fell as large programs piled up. The pandemic years fall inside that period, so the numbers should be read with care.Put together, the evidence says people are most sensitive to change that is large, sudden, imposed, or piled on. This paper found no study that measures delay itself. But a change that has been put off for years tends to arrive in exactly this form. It is large because the need has grown. It is sudden because a crisis forces it. It is imposed because there is no longer time to involve anyone.Opinion 7. That suggests a loop, and this paper offers it as a proposal. A leader expects resistance and puts off small changes. The need grows. The change finally arrives large and sudden. People react badly. The leader takes the reaction as proof that people resist change. The belief is confirmed by the delay it caused.The next part turns to what the late options cost, and to what a durable organization does instead.Part Six: What a durable organization doesThe argument so far comes to this. Strength and durability are different qualities. This paper argues that change is safest when it is small and on time, and most dangerous when it is large and late. Change comes late mostly because leaders do not see the need, and partly because they expect more of a fight than the evidence predicts.The lesson follows. Watch the organization’s vital signs, and do not judge its health by its strength on paper. Make changes at the speed the organization requires. Do not let them build up until the only tools left are the largest ones.Opinion 8. In practice that means four things. They are recommendations, and they go beyond what the studies prove.First, read the vital signs. Ask the people who do the work what is going wrong, and make it safe to answer. Nokia’s top managers were not short of information. They were short of true information. Treat the departure of able people as a signal, and ask what they saw. Look at how decisions and work really move, and put that picture in front of the people who run the organization. The Unmeasured Layer proposes what that picture should cover.Second, set a regular rhythm for looking. The need for change is hard to see, so a durable organization does not wait until it is obvious. It reviews its own condition on a schedule, changes what the review finds, and leaves the rest alone. This is how a regular pace and the speed of need fit together. The regular review is what keeps the organization from falling behind the need. Nothing Stays Tuned describes such a practice. It starts with the commitments that matter most and get the weakest feedback. It uses calendar dates as checkpoints, and it adds a review when conditions change or warning signs appear.Third, make changes while they are small, and give the people affected a part in them. The research in Part Five points to both. People accept change more readily when they see a benefit in it and have a hand in it.Fourth, treat the largest tools as last resorts, and know what they cost.Reorganizations are the first of these. In a McKinsey survey of 1,800 executives, about two thirds of reorganizations delivered at least some improvement. But more than 80 percent failed to deliver the value they were meant to in the time planned, and about 10 percent did real damage to the company. The same article reports that productivity fell noticeably in about 60 percent of cases. A Bain study of 57 reorganizations found that fewer than a third produced any meaningful gain in performance.Large layoffs are the second. They are also the easiest of these tools to reach for. Fast to Cut, Slow to Build noted that a large company can plan a round of layoffs in a few weeks. The costs come later. In a study of 200 companies by Charlie Trevor and Anthony Nyberg, downsizing was followed by more voluntary quitting. In some cases the people who quit afterward far outnumbered the people laid off. The cost is not only to the company. A Finnish study followed 22,430 public employees who kept their jobs through a period of cuts. Where the cuts were largest, sick leave rose among permanent staff, and deaths from cardiovascular disease were about twice as frequent as where there were little or no cuts. And in the study of declining firms described in Part Three, early layoffs did not improve the odds of recovery.Company-wide transformations are the third. Here this paper gives no number, because the number usually quoted is the 70 percent figure, which has no valid and reliable evidence behind it.These tools share one thing. Each is large, sudden, and imposed. Each is the form that change takes when it has been put off.Sometimes a large and urgent change cannot be avoided. A shock comes from outside, or the need was truly hidden, or a new leader inherits years of delay. Then the change should be made, and made without illusions. The larger study of declining firms found that, once a firm was in decline, acting with urgency generally went with better results. But the risks described in this paper still apply. A change raises the chance of failure for a time, and a large one is hard on the people inside. Urgency does not remove those costs. It only makes them worth paying.This paper has limits, and they should be stated plainly.The link between states and companies is an analogy. The pattern in states is a tendency and not a law. Some authoritarian systems have made very large changes and lasted, and single-party regimes in particular have proved long-lived. The forecasting study covers 1955 to 2003, and a later test of its model found that its accuracy varied over time. So the regime findings describe a period. They are not a permanent ranking.The studies of organizations are few. Each covers one kind of organization in one period, and each shows what went together, not what caused what. The three company cases illustrate the argument. They were chosen because of how they ended, so they cannot prove it.Two claims are this paper’s own judgment. One is that not seeing is more common than refusing. The other is the loop in Part Five, in which the belief in resistance causes the delay that seems to confirm it.One limit needs its own note. Changing on time does not guarantee that a change lasts. The best long-run evidence comes from a study of Indian weaving firms. Nine years after an experiment that improved their management practices, about half of the practices the plants had adopted had been dropped, though those plants still used more of the practices than the comparison plants in the experiment. The reasons given most often were that managers had moved on and that directors lacked the time. This paper’s author is currently researching what causes change to fade. The studies available have limits. Few follow an organization for many years after a change. In a first review of 78 records, the author found three studies that clearly met the review’s rules. None of the three recorded, before the work began, who inside the organization stood to lose from it. Until that is better understood, the advice in this paper comes with a caution. Change made on time is better than change made late. It still has to be kept.The claim that remains is the one this paper began with. A system can be strong and still be brittle. What makes an organization durable is not how firmly it holds its position. It is whether it can see its own condition and change while the change is still small. A durable organization keeps its vital signs in view and changes at the speed of need.ReferencesOn earlier papers by the author named in the text: The Unmeasured Layer: Administrative Reality and the Risk Standard Reporting Misses; Nothing Stays Tuned; Fast to Cut, Slow to Build: Why Big Companies Get Stuck; Trenches, Not Silos: The Bill for Dividing a Company; Hold, Collide, Come Apart: The Three Forms the Trenches Take; and We Gave Bureaucracy the Fastest Tools in History. It Got Slower.On regime stability: Jack A. Goldstone and others, “A Global Model for Forecasting Political Instability,” American Journal of Political Science 54, no. 1 (2010): 190–208. For the later test of that model: Drew Bowlsby, Erica Chenoweth, Cullen Hendrix, and Jonathan D. Moyer, “The Future Is a Moving Target: Predicting Political Instability,” British Journal of Political Science, 2020.On leaders and regimes in autocracies: Barbara Geddes, Joseph Wright, and Erica Frantz, “Autocratic Breakdown and Regime Transitions: A New Data Set,” Perspectives on Politics 12, no. 2 (2014): 313–331.On strong and brittle rule: Stephen Kotkin, interviewed by David Remnick, “The Weakness of the Despot,” The New Yorker, March 11, 2022. Stephen Kotkin, Armageddon Averted: The Soviet Collapse, 1970–2000 (Oxford University Press, 2001). Alexis de Tocqueville, The Old Regime and the Revolution (1856).On Digital Equipment: Edgar H. Schein, with Peter S. DeLisi, Paul J. Kampas, and Michael M. Sonduck, DEC Is Dead, Long Live DEC: The Lasting Legacy of Digital Equipment Corporation (Berrett-Koehler, 2003). For sales and the sale to Compaq: Associated Press, obituary of Kenneth Olsen, February 2011.On Nokia: Nokia Corporation, second quarter 2008 results, stock exchange release, July 17, 2008, for the company’s estimate of its market share. Associated Press, “Nokia, Microsoft complete $7.5B cellphone deal,” April 25, 2014. Timo O. Vuori and Quy N. Huy, “Distributed Attention and Shared Emotions in the Innovation Process: How Nokia Lost the Smartphone Battle,” Administrative Science Quarterly 61, no. 1 (2016): 9–51.On General Motors: The New York Times, “After Many Stumbles, the Fall of an American Giant,” June 2009, for the 1954 share. CNN Money, “GM’s No. 1 sales title at risk,” June 4, 2009, for the 2004 and 2009 shares. Bloomberg News, April 24, 2009, for losses since 2004. Al Jazeera, June 2, 2009, for the length of the decline. General Motors, “The New General Motors Company Launches Today,” news release, July 10, 2009, for the core brands, the workforce, the obligations, and the ownership. 24/7 Wall St., July 10, 2009, for the brands dropped. The Globe and Mail, “GM’s bumpy ride,” for the dates and the public money.On change and the risk of failure: Terry L. Amburgey, Dawn Kelly, and William P. Barnett, “Resetting the Clock: The Dynamics of Organizational Change and Failure,” Administrative Science Quarterly 38, no. 1 (1993): 51–73. On failing top teams: Donald C. Hambrick and Richard A. D’Aveni, “Top Team Deterioration as Part of the Downward Spiral of Large Corporate Bankruptcies,” Management Science 38, no. 10 (1992): 1445–1466.On suppressed volatility: Nassim Nicholas Taleb and Mark Blyth, “The Black Swan of Cairo,” Foreign Affairs, May/June 2011. For the forest image: Taleb, interviewed by Walter Kemp, “Risk and Robustness,” IPI Global Observatory, September 2014. Nassim Nicholas Taleb and Gregory F. Treverton, “The Calm Before the Storm,” Foreign Affairs, January/February 2015. Paul B. Stares, “Warning Signs: A Response to ‘The Calm Before the Storm,’” Foreign Affairs, 2015.On resilience in companies: Gary Hamel and Liisa Välikangas, “The Quest for Resilience,” Harvard Business Review 81, no. 9 (September 2003): 52–63. For Hamel’s phrase: Kathleen Melymuka, “Resilience: Staying On Top,” Computerworld, September 8, 2003.On rhythm and timing: Patricia Klarner and Sebastian Raisch, “Move to the Beat: Rhythms of Change and Firm Performance,” Academy of Management Journal 56, no. 1 (2013): 160–184. Chanchai Tangpong, Michael Abebe, and Zonghui Li, “A Temporal Approach to Retrenchment and Successful Turnaround in Declining Firms,” Journal of Management Studies 52, no. 5 (2015): 647–677. José Luis Barbero, Filippo Di Pietro, and C. Chiang, “A Rush of Blood to the Head: Temporal Dimensions of Retrenchment, Environment and Turnaround Performance,” Long Range Planning 50, no. 6 (2017): 862–879.On decline, silence, and exit: William Weitzel and Ellen Jonsson, “Decline in Organizations: A Literature Integration and Extension,” Administrative Science Quarterly 34, no. 1 (1989): 91–109. Frances J. Milliken, Elizabeth W. Morrison, and Patricia F. Hewlin, “An Exploratory Study of Employee Silence,” Journal of Management Studies 40, no. 6 (2003): 1453–1476. Albert O. Hirschman, Exit, Voice, and Loyalty (Harvard University Press, 1970). Elizabeth Anderson, Private Government (Princeton University Press, 2017).On resistance to change: Eric B. Dent and Susan Galloway Goldberg, “Challenging ‘Resistance to Change,’” Journal of Applied Behavioral Science 35, no. 1 (1999): 25–41. Lester Coch and John R. P. French, Jr., “Overcoming Resistance to Change,” Human Relations 1, no. 4 (1948): 512–532. Bernard Burnes, “Understanding Resistance to Change: Building on Coch and French,” Journal of Change Management 15, no. 2 (2015): 92–116. Alexandra Michel, Rune Todnem By, and Bernard Burnes, “The Limitations of Dispositional Resistance in Relation to Organizational Change,” Management Decision 51, no. 4 (2013): 761–780. Shaul Oreg, “Resistance to Change: Developing an Individual Differences Measure,” Journal of Applied Psychology 88, no. 4 (2003): 680–693.On the 70 percent figure: Mark Hughes, “Do 70 Per Cent of All Organizational Change Initiatives Really Fail?” Journal of Change Management 11, no. 4 (2011): 451–464, which traces the figure to Michael Hammer and James Champy, Reengineering the Corporation (1993).On the pull of the familiar: William Samuelson and Richard Zeckhauser, “Status Quo Bias in Decision Making,” Journal of Risk and Uncertainty 1 (1988): 7–59. Qinyu Xiao, Choi Shan Lam, Muhrajan Piara, and Gilad Feldman, “Revisiting Status Quo Bias: Replication of Samuelson and Zeckhauser (1988),” Meta-Psychology 5 (2021).On how employees react to change: Alannah E. Rafferty and Mark A. Griffin, “Perceptions of Organizational Change: A Stress and Coping Perspective,” Journal of Applied Psychology 91, no. 5 (2006): 1154–1162. Gartner research, as reported in “Employees Are Losing Patience with Change Initiatives,” Harvard Business Review, 2023.On reorganizations: Stephen Heidari-Robinson and Suzanne Heywood, “Getting Reorgs Right,” Harvard Business Review, November 2016. Marcia W. Blenko, Michael C. Mankins, and Paul Rogers, “The Decision-Driven Organization,” Harvard Business Review, June 2010.On layoffs: Charlie O. Trevor and Anthony J. Nyberg, “Keeping Your Headcount When All About You Are Losing Theirs,” Academy of Management Journal 51, no. 2 (2008): 259–276. Jussi Vahtera, Mika Kivimäki, and others, “Organisational Downsizing, Sickness Absence, and Mortality: 10-Town Prospective Cohort Study,” BMJ 328 (2004): 555.On whether improvements last: Nicholas Bloom, Aprajit Mahajan, David McKenzie, and John Roberts, “Do Management Interventions Last? Evidence from India,” American Economic Journal: Applied Economics 12, no. 2 (2020): 198–219."}, {"title": "Wrong Date, Wrong Institution, Wrong Scale", "subtitle": "Three careful answers about AI, and where each was aimed", "topic": "Institutions & trust", "url": "/essays/wrong-date-wrong-institution-wrong/", "text": "Three answers, each a little off.Bloomberg recently aired a report by Chrystia Freeland on what artificial intelligence means for people. It was not about jobs, and it was not about the end of the world. Three people spoke: Michael Sandel, a philosopher at Harvard; Daniel Diermeier, the chancellor of Vanderbilt University; and Meghan Sullivan, a philosopher at Notre Dame.All three were careful, and all three asked a serious question. This essay does not think any of them is foolish. It thinks each answer, as given in the segment, was aimed a little off. One has the wrong date. One leans on the wrong institution. One works at the wrong scale. The misses matter, because where you place a problem decides where you look for the remedy.A note on fairness comes first. Each of the three has said more elsewhere than a few minutes of television allows. This essay answers what was said in the segment.The wrong dateSandel said the real question is not whether AI will destroy humanity. It is whether AI will change what it means to be human. He fears a world where people lose presence, place, and real friendship, and where bots stand in for friends.The fear is sound. The timing is off. He named no date, but he spoke in the future tense, and he gave AI as the cause.Much of that loss was measured before the chatbots arrived. In 2023 the U.S. Surgeon General reported that Americans spent about 20 minutes a day in person with friends in 2020, down from about 60 in 2003. For people aged 15 to 24, time with friends fell by nearly 70 percent over the same period. The last year in that count was a pandemic year, which makes the fall look steeper than the long trend. But the count ends in 2020. ChatGPT was released at the end of November 2022.These numbers count hours. They do not measure the thing Sandel fears most, which is a person replaced by a simulation. That part is new, and he is right to name it. But the hours were already going.Nor was the machine absent in those years. In 2016 Facebook’s engineers wrote that machine learning ranked and personalized the stories in its News Feed. A machine was already choosing what people saw of one another, years before one could hold a conversation.Opinion 1. Here is the first of this essay’s opinions. Sandel has the loss right and the timing wrong. In this essay’s view, the phone and the feed did much of the damage, and they did it first. A bot that takes the place of the person at the other end can make it worse. But rules for chatbots alone will not repair a loss that was under way before the chatbots.The wrong institutionDiermeier said students need to be “bilingual.” They need to be at ease with the technology, and they need to be able to think for themselves. He said the purpose of a university education is to help students develop their own minds.That claim is right. It is close to the test offered in an earlier essay here, “The Truth Will Set You Free”: does this use of the machine leave people able to understand, to check, and to answer for a decision?His answer was about students. A chancellor speaks for a university, so that is natural, and a university offers more than instruction. What is off is how far the answer reaches. Most of the people who will learn from these machines are not enrolled anywhere.They can now get something no library gave them. A library gives access. It does not teach. A machine that answers one question at a time, at any hour, comes much closer to a tutor, and it is open to anyone. Whether it helps depends on how it is built. In a study of nearly a thousand high school students in Turkey, a chatbot that simply gave answers left students worse off once it was taken away. A version built to give hints, with guidance from teachers written in, did not have that effect. The study does not show that a chatbot alone can educate a person. It shows that the design decides whether the tool does harm. And a tool can be designed by anyone who builds one, not only by a university.There is also the matter of proof. A degree is a witness. It says the learning took place. That is a separate question, and this essay leaves it for another day.Opinion 2. The second opinion is this. Diermeier is right about what people need, and for his own students his answer may be enough. But most people will meet these machines outside any campus. Nobody in the segment said who will teach them to think for themselves, or who will check the tools that teach them. That is the larger question, and it does not belong to universities alone.The wrong scaleSullivan made two points. The first was about pressure. She said some reports put the investment in data centers and AI training at about 2 percent of the country’s economy. A bet of that size, she said, creates pressure to push the technology out as widely as possible, perhaps before the ethical and safety work is done.The second was about agency. She hopes that competition among the companies, and the consciences of users in large numbers, will press them to choose well. She said responsibility does not belong only to a few executives and regulators. Many of us decide what AI we allow into our workplaces, our schools, and our homes, and those in power would like us to feel that we are just “along for the ride.”She is right that fatalism is wrong, and she is right that users can move these companies. In April 2025, OpenAI withdrew an update to its assistant after users complained that it had become too eager to agree with them.What is off is the scale, in three ways.First, the pressure is not only a market. In August 2025, China’s State Council set targets for intelligent devices and AI agents to pass 70 percent penetration by 2027 and 90 percent by 2030. Pressure from users works on companies that compete for them. It has far less pull on a state plan.Second, what users reward in large numbers is not always what protects them. The same OpenAI case shows it. By the company’s own account, a training signal built from users’ thumbs-up and thumbs-down ratings likely helped make the assistant too agreeable in the first place. Users corrected the problem. Their approval also helped cause it.Third, there is a risk in any appeal to users. In 2004 the oil company BP put a carbon footprint calculator on its website and went on to popularize the term. Critics have argued that the effect was to move attention from the companies to the individual. Sullivan is not doing that. She wants more people to carry the responsibility, not different people. The risk is that an appeal to users becomes a substitute for limits on the organizations doing the deploying.Opinion 3. This is the third opinion. Sullivan describes pressure on the scale of a national economy, and her answer rests on the choices of users. Seeing that a use is wrong is not the same as having the authority to refuse it. A nurse, a teacher, or a new employee may see the problem and have no power to act on it. “The Truth Will Set You Free” argued that announcements rarely change what people are rewarded for doing. Appeals to conscience are not a substitute for rules and rewards that let people act on it.Where to aimNone of this makes the three speakers wrong about what is at stake. Sandel is right that something human is being lost. Diermeier is right about what a person needs. Sullivan is right that people are not helpless.But each answer, as given, sends the remedy to too narrow a place. Rules for chatbots alone will not repair a loss that was under way before the chatbots. A university’s answer does not reach the people who are not enrolled in one. And the choices of users, even in large numbers, do not match pressure on the scale of a national economy or a state plan.The remedies belong where the problems are. That means the phone and the feed as well as the bot. It means whoever builds the tools that teach. And it means institutions that can write a rule, check it, and reward people for keeping it. Institutions feel the same pressure that individuals do. The difference is that an institution can bind itself. A hospital can refuse to buy a tool until someone other than the seller has tested it. A school can decide which uses of a machine count as a student’s own work. A person acting alone can do neither. This is not an argument against the tools. It is an argument about where to aim.One more thing should be said. A Harvard philosopher, a university chancellor, and a Notre Dame philosopher took up this question on a business news program. That is a sign of how large a force AI has already become, and it is an important development. AI should not simply happen to us without a public argument about it, and this author welcomes theirs. A public argument includes polite disagreement. This essay is offered as one.ReferencesBloomberg. Report by Chrystia Freeland with Michael Sandel, Daniel Diermeier, and Meghan Sullivan, October 2026. Video and excerpts at RealClearPolitics, 5 October 2026.U.S. Surgeon General. “Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation: The U.S. Surgeon General’s Advisory on the Healing Effects of Social Connection and Community.” 2023.OpenAI. “Introducing ChatGPT.” 30 November 2022.Dunn, Jeffrey. “Introducing FBLearner Flow: Facebook’s AI backbone.” Engineering at Meta, 9 May 2016.John Elijah, “The Truth Will Set You Free,” October 2026.Bastani, Hamsa, Osbert Bastani, Alp Sungu, Haosen Ge, Özge Kabakcı, and Rei Mariman. “Generative AI without guardrails can harm learning: Evidence from high school mathematics.” Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 122(26), e2422633122 (2025).OpenAI. “Sycophancy in GPT-4o: What happened and what we’re doing about it,” 29 April 2025, and “Expanding on what we missed with sycophancy,” 2 May 2025.State Council of China. Guideline on the “AI Plus” initiative, as reported on the Chinese government’s English-language site, 27 August 2025.Kaufman, Mark. “The carbon footprint sham.” Mashable, July 2020."}, {"title": "The Truth Will Set You Free", "subtitle": "AI and the Promise Over the University Door", "topic": "Culture & ideas", "url": "/essays/the-truth-will-set-you-free/", "text": "The Main Building at the University of Texas at Austin. Photo: Larry D. Moore, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. Cropped, converted to black and white, and marked...At the University of Texas at Austin, a sentence is carved in stone above the south entrance of the Main Building, the one with the Tower. It reads: Ye shall know the truth and the truth shall make you free. The author of this essay studied there. He saw that inscription for four years, and he took it at face value. By getting a college education, he was doing something good in the service of progress.Johns Hopkins University adopted the same sentence in Latin as its motto in 1885: Veritas vos liberabit. The California Institute of Technology uses it in English. Other universities use it too, in other countries and other languages.This essay makes one argument in three steps. The motto promises freedom to the person who does the knowing. The rule it became, pursue knowledge, has no stopping point, even when better tools begin to do the knowing in that person’s place. So the pursuit can end up working against its own purpose.This is not the common worry that AI will make people less capable. The argument holds even if people stay just as capable and the machine is right every time. A person who cannot check an answer has to take it on authority, and the motto promised the opposite.From the three steps the essay draws a test for any use of artificial intelligence. Does it leave people able to understand, to check, and to answer for a decision? The separate points are not new, and the essay credits the people who made them. What it offers is the connection between them, and the test.The sentence is older than any of these schools. It comes from the Gospel of John, chapter 8, verse 32. In the King James translation it reads: “And ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free.”This essay is not preaching, and it does not ask anyone to follow Jesus. Jesus and the Gospel of John are used here as historical anchor points, to show where the sentence came from and what it first meant.The three schools above quote only the promise. Yonsei University in Seoul quotes the verse before it as well. In the Gospel, Jesus is speaking to people who had come to believe him, and the sentence begins one verse earlier with a condition: “If ye continue in my word, then are ye my disciples indeed.” The promise follows from that condition. His listeners object. They say they have never been slaves to anyone, so how can they be made free? He answers that whoever commits sin is the servant of sin.So the passage is specific about its terms. The truth is tied to continuing in a teaching. It is something a person lives by, and it is more than a set of facts a person holds. The freedom is freedom from sin. It is not freedom from a king, and it is not freedom from ignorance.This reading is not new. In 1978 the biologist Max Delbrück told Caltech’s graduating class where their motto came from. He said the verse promised freedom from sin, and that the man who chose it for Caltech meant something different by it.One more detail matters, and it is easy to pass over. The promise is made to “ye,” the people listening. The people who will be made free are the people who do the knowing. The knowing is theirs to do.On campus, the sentence came to carry a different meaning. There was no single day when this happened. Over a few centuries, truth came to mean knowledge: what observation, experiment, and argument can establish. Freedom came to mean what knowledge provides: relief from ignorance, from superstition, from disease, and from want.Francis Bacon gave this meaning some of its best-known words. In 1605 he wrote that knowledge should serve “the glory of the Creator and the relief of man’s estate,” which means the improvement of the human condition. A line he wrote in 1597 is remembered as “knowledge is power.” Where he wrote it, the line was about the power of God. Later generations took it as a statement about human power over nature.In 1784 Immanuel Kant gave the Enlightenment a motto of its own: “Dare to know.” He explained it this way: “Have the courage to use your own understanding.”This became the promise of the modern university. Pursue knowledge, because knowledge is good in itself and because it is how people and societies move forward. A large part of that promise has been kept. People live longer than they did. Fewer children die. Work that once broke bodies is done by machines. Much of that came from the pursuit the motto describes.The new meaning changed the truth, and it changed the freedom. It kept one thing from the original. The person still had to do the knowing. A student who memorizes the answer key has not learned the subject. A person who is handed a conclusion and told to accept it has not been freed by the truth. On this point the Gospel and the Enlightenment agree, each for its own reasons.That is this essay’s reading of how the meaning moved. The texts and the dates are on the record. The reading is offered as a reading.The motto tells people to pursue knowledge. It does not say how far. It also does not say what to do if the pursuit produces something that can do the knowing in their place.“Pursue knowledge” is a rule for going. It names a direction. It does not name a place to stop.Most founding principles are like this. They are written by people who are trying to get something started, against resistance. They say what to do. They rarely say when to stop doing it. For a long time that causes no trouble, because the resistance does the stopping. There were once authorities that claimed the right to say what should not be studied. Over several centuries they lost that argument, and that was a real gain.Winning has a cost that shows up later. When the opposition is gone, few are left whose job is to ask how far. And inside the winning side, credit goes to the people who apply the principle most fully. A career in research is built on finding something new. There is no prize for deciding not to look.There are limits in some places. Research on people generally requires their informed consent. Some weapons are banned by treaty. These rules were mostly written after harm was done, and they restrict methods and uses in particular fields. The general rule stayed where it was: finding out more is good.The author has called this pattern terminal fidelity. A principle is followed so faithfully, for so long, that it ends up producing the opposite of what it was for. The principle is not false, and the people following it are not acting in bad faith. The trouble is in the missing stopping rule.The idea has relatives. Ivan Illich argued in 1973 that tools serve people only within limits, and that past those limits they make people dependent.Apply that to the motto. If knowledge is good without limit, then better tools for getting knowledge are good too. The telescope and the microscope extended what people could see. The computer took over calculation, then storage, then search. Each tool did a part of the knowing that people used to do themselves. The next tool in that line is one that does the reasoning and the discovering.That is how the people building artificial intelligence describe their goal. The head of one AI company has written that these systems could compress fifty to a hundred years of progress in biology into five to ten. Another has called scientific progress “the biggest driver of overall progress.” They are describing the university’s promise, delivered by a machine.Opinion 1. Here is the first of this essay’s opinions, marked as an opinion. A machine that is better than people at finding things out is what the word superintelligence means here. Such a machine looks like the natural result of the pursuit the motto describes. It is where “pursue knowledge” leads when nothing says stop. Natural does not mean necessary. It took money, competition between companies, and rivalry between countries to build these systems, and truth-seeking alone did not do it. And no such machine exists today. What exists is a trajectory.What can be observed today is dependence.Many people would struggle to get through a day without a phone. It holds their directions, their money, their tickets, and their contacts. Most of that dependence is ordinary. Most people depend on others for their food and their electricity, and that does not have to make them less free.Some studies point at a cost. A 2020 study of fifty drivers found that the more a person had relied on GPS over the years, the worse that person did at finding the way without it. A study published in 2025 worked with nearly a thousand students at a high school in Turkey. Some of them got an AI tutor for math practice. They did better while they had it. When it was taken away, they did worse than students who had never used it. A version of the tutor that gave hints, and withheld answers, avoided most of that harm.These studies have limits. One tested fifty people and retested thirteen of them about three years later. The other covered one subject at one school over a short period. They suggest that handing a tool the work can weaken the skill. They do not show that people in general are becoming less capable, and they say nothing about machines that do not exist yet. Fears like this are also old. Socrates worried that writing would weaken memory.That is the worry most people have heard: the tools will make people less capable. This essay’s argument does not depend on it. Suppose the skills hold. Suppose every student keeps learning the math. The harder problem is still there, because it does not come from people getting worse. It comes from the machine getting better than anyone can check.Opinion 2. The second opinion concerns who holds the knowledge. Power and knowledge are different things. Over time they drift toward each other. Knowledge that gives an advantage attracts money, and money buys the equipment and the people that produce more knowledge. Nobody has to intend it. C.S. Lewis made the point in 1943: power over nature ends up as power that some people hold over other people.The record in this field fits. According to Stanford’s AI Index, universities produced the most notable AI systems each year until 2014. By 2025, companies produced more than 90 percent of them. In 2011, new AI PhDs in the United States and Canada split about evenly between universities and industry. By 2022, about 70 percent went to industry and 20 percent stayed in universities. The same index reports that the most capable systems are now concentrated in a small number of organizations, and that they are the least open to outside inspection. The drift is not total. In 2024, universities produced more than two thirds of the AI research papers the index counted.Opinion 3. The third opinion concerns why the path is hard to leave. Relying on someone who knows more is rational. The philosopher John Hardwig argued in 1985 that modern knowledge already works this way, because no one can check everything personally. The same holds for a machine that gives better answers than a person can find alone. Using it is the sensible choice.That is the difficulty. The economist Brian Arthur described in 1989 how sensible choices can lock a society into a path. Each person who takes the better answer can make the next person’s choice easier, and can make going without the tool more costly. The skill that would let people do without it is practiced less, and over time fewer people may have it. No one has to make a mistake for this to happen. Each step can be reasonable, and together the steps can make the path harder to reverse.Now put the pieces next to the sentence over the door.“Ye shall know the truth.” In both of its meanings, the old one and the campus one, the promise depends on the person doing the knowing. A machine that finds things out better than people can changes that. The truth can then be known without the person knowing it. The machine holds the reasons. The person receives the answer. This would be true even if the answer were right every time. It would be true for the most capable person alive.When people cannot check an answer, they accept it on authority. That is the position the Enlightenment set out to end. “Dare to know” meant: stop taking the truth on someone else’s word. A true answer taken on trust may still count as knowing. It is not the knowing the motto promised, which was the person’s own.Opinion 4. This is the fourth opinion, and it is the center of the essay. On the present trajectory, the pursuit of knowledge leads back to authority. A principle meant to make people knowers ends by making them receivers. That is the pattern named earlier. The faithful application arrives at the opposite of the purpose.Others have reached this point by different roads. Max Horkheimer and Theodor Adorno argued in the 1940s that knowledge pursued to free people can become a means of control. Hannah Arendt warned in 1958 that separating technical knowledge from thought could leave people “at the mercy of every gadget.” Henry Kissinger wrote in 2018 that artificial intelligence could bring the Enlightenment to an end, in part because a machine may reach conclusions it cannot explain. The psychologist Mike Brooks recently used the same inscription in Austin to argue that people should tell AI systems to put truth first.Each of these concerns has a history. This essay connects three points. The promise is made to the person who does the knowing. The rule has no stopping point. So faithful pursuit can undo the promise. That reversal is what the author calls terminal fidelity.Three risks follow. They are risks, and none of them is a prediction.The first is concentrated power. Whoever owns a machine that knows more than everyone else holds an advantage over everyone else. The last twenty years offer a smaller version. A handful of companies came to decide the order in which a large share of humanity sees news, video, and search results. Many social media feeds rank posts by predicted engagement, which means clicks, likes, and shares. A 2025 study of one platform found that this kind of ranking showed people more angry and divisive political posts than a plain time-ordered feed did. The users themselves did not prefer the political posts it chose. What draws engagement and what is true are different things.The second is information presented as truth when it has been shaped by something else. In April 2025, one of the largest AI companies withdrew an update to its assistant. By the company’s own account, the update had made the assistant too eager to agree with its users. An answer machine can be tuned, on purpose or by accident, and the person asking has no easy way to see the tuning.The third is harm when answers are trusted without checking. A public database now lists more than two thousand legal decisions in which a court found, or suspected, that someone relied on false material produced by an AI system. Most often the material was a case citation that did not exist. Those are the cases where someone checked.The other side deserves its due. The same machines may deliver what Bacon hoped for: faster cures, less drudgery, less want. The physicist David Deutsch has argued that problems are solvable through the growth of knowledge, and that this growth has no natural limit. Design also matters. The tutor that gave hints did not weaken its students the way the tutor that gave answers did. And this essay is not an argument against using these systems. The author uses them.The original passage offers one more anchor, used here as history and not as instruction. The listeners in John insist that they have never been slaves to anyone. The reply is that a person can be in bondage without knowing it. That is a fair description of how dependence works. It does not feel like chains. It feels like convenience. “Slaves” is too strong a word for anything the evidence shows today. “Dependents” is closer, and dependents are not free in the sense the motto promised.Opinion 5. So what would a stopping rule look like? This essay does not have one to hand down, and it doubts that a rule announced from above would hold. Announcements rarely change what people are rewarded for doing. What it can offer is where the line might sit, as its last opinion. The motto carries its own test. A use of the machine that leaves people able to understand, to check, and to answer for a decision keeps the promise. A use that replaces those things breaks it, however good the answers are.Does this use of the machine leave people able to understand, to check, and to answer for a decision?Whether the trajectory ever reaches a machine that out-knows people is not known. Whether people will keep the knowing where it matters is not known either. Both questions are open.The sentence is still over the door in Austin. It is worth reading again from its first three words. Ye shall know.ReferencesUniversity of Texas at Austin. Inscription above the south entrance of the Main Building. See Danielle Brune Sigler, “From buildings to books to tattoos,” UT News, 27 February 2012.Johns Hopkins University. Motto adopted in 1885. See “The truth will set you free,” Johns Hopkins Magazine, Winter 2017.California Institute of Technology. Motto, “The truth shall make you free.” Caltech Archives, record of the Institute seal.The Holy Bible, King James Version, John 8:31-36.Yonsei University. Founding philosophy, quoting John 8:31-32. About Yonsei, yonsei.ac.kr.Delbrück, Max. “The Arrow of Time: Beginning and End.” Engineering and Science 42, no. 1 (September-October 1978): 5-9. Caltech commencement address.Bacon, Francis. The Advancement of Learning (1605), Book I.Bacon, Francis. Meditationes Sacrae (1597), “Of Heresies.”Kant, Immanuel. “An Answer to the Question: What Is Enlightenment?” Berlinische Monatsschrift, December 1784. Translated by Mary C. Smith.Illich, Ivan. Tools for Conviviality. Harper & Row, 1973.Amodei, Dario. “Machines of Loving Grace.” October 2024.Altman, Sam. “The Gentle Singularity.” 10 June 2025.Dahmani, Louisa, and Veronique D. Bohbot. “Habitual use of GPS negatively impacts spatial memory during self-guided navigation.” Scientific Reports 10, article 6310 (2020).Bastani, Hamsa, Osbert Bastani, Alp Sungu, Haosen Ge, Özge Kabakcı, and Rei Mariman. “Generative AI without guardrails can harm learning: Evidence from high school mathematics.” Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 122(26), e2422633122 (2025).Plato. Phaedrus, 274c-275b.Lewis, C.S. The Abolition of Man. Oxford University Press, 1943. HarperOne edition, 2001, page 55.Stanford Institute for Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence. AI Index Report 2024, chapter 6; AI Index Report 2025, chapter 1; AI Index Report 2026, chapter 1.Hardwig, John. “Epistemic Dependence.” The Journal of Philosophy 82, no. 7 (July 1985): 335-349.Arthur, W. Brian. “Competing Technologies, Increasing Returns, and Lock-In by Historical Events.” The Economic Journal 99, no. 394 (March 1989): 116-131.Horkheimer, Max, and Theodor W. Adorno. Dialectic of Enlightenment: Philosophical Fragments. Translated by Edmund Jephcott. Stanford University Press, 2002. First published 1947.Arendt, Hannah. The Human Condition. University of Chicago Press, 1958. Second edition, 1998, page 3.Kissinger, Henry A. “How the Enlightenment Ends.” The Atlantic, June 2018.Brooks, Mike. “How to Use AI to Seek Truth Over Tribe and Ego.” Psychology Today, updated 18 September 2026.Milli, Smitha, Micah Carroll, Yike Wang, Sashrika Pandey, Sebastian Zhao, and Anca D. Dragan. “Engagement, user satisfaction, and the amplification of divisive content on social media.” PNAS Nexus 4(3), pgaf062 (2025).OpenAI. “Sycophancy in GPT-4o: What happened and what we’re doing about it,” 29 April 2025, and “Expanding on what we missed with sycophancy,” 2 May 2025.Charlotin, Damien. AI Hallucination Cases Database, damiencharlotin.com/hallucinations. 2,145 cases as of 4 October 2026.Deutsch, David. The Beginning of Infinity: Explanations That Transform the World. Allen Lane, 2011."}];